We'll continue.

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:30.130
So we'll continue.

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:35.560
Last time we looked at this
binary hypothesis testing,

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:36.520
which is--

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:40.954
in some ways, it's the
essence of all of statistics.

00:02:40.954 --> 00:02:44.110
You have-- in this case, you
have just two distributions.

00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:45.850
You observe one sample,
and the question

00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:49.820
is, which distribution
generated it, one or the other?

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:52.090
And if you make that
more complicated

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:55.090
and have distributions not
on a finite number of points

00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:59.590
but on Rn or something like
that or more complicated things

00:02:59.590 --> 00:03:02.020
and you have more
than two hypotheses,

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:05.410
then that's kind of most
of statistics right there.

00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:09.230
But anyway, so this is
a very simple setting.

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:14.380
And so the question was to
make a guess about whether--

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:16.480
an observation,
and an observation

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:18.280
is like a number like 3.

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:20.020
That's an observation.

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:21.700
That's what happened.

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:24.650
And then your goal is to try
to figure out, did it come--

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:26.060
or guess in some
intelligent way,

00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:29.030
did it come from this
distribution or this one?

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:31.700
And we looked at that.

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:36.230
You end up with-- you have this
idea of a randomized detector.

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:39.620
It's a policy that
says when you observe

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:43.950
this outcome, which distribution
do you attribute it to?

00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:50.330
And if it's-- and that you
do with a 2 by n matrix where

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:53.510
the columns give you the
probabilities that you announce

00:03:53.510 --> 00:03:57.890
it came from p or q, the
two different distributions.

00:03:57.890 --> 00:04:00.740
So that's how that works.

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:03.650
If those are all 0s and 1s,
then it's deterministic.

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:08.210
And you end up-- you write
this down as a bicriterion

00:04:08.210 --> 00:04:09.770
optimization problem.

00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:11.870
There's two things
you care about,

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:14.030
the probability of
a false-positive--

00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:16.190
and that's Pfp--

00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:18.726
and the probability
of a false-negative.

00:04:18.726 --> 00:04:20.309
And so-- and these
are just-- you just

00:04:20.309 --> 00:04:21.860
get these from matrix
multiplication.

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:23.318
And the point is
that each of these

00:04:23.318 --> 00:04:30.980
is a linear function in T. T is
what describes your detector.

00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:33.200
Anyway, so this
problem, it turns out,

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:37.850
is actually really quite
straightforward to solve.

00:04:37.850 --> 00:04:42.920
If you scalarize it, so you say
that lambda now is the amount--

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:45.020
is the factor that
relates how much I

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:48.290
care about false-positives
versus false-negatives

00:04:48.290 --> 00:04:50.660
or something like
that, then it turns out

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:54.350
it just has a super-simple
solution that looks like this.

00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:56.490
Lambda is that weighting factor.

00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:59.600
For example, if it's 1,
it says, actually, I just

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:03.000
want to minimize the
number of errors, period.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:04.440
That would be lambda equals 1.

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:06.193
And then the
solution is analytic,

00:05:06.193 --> 00:05:07.860
and it's been known
since, I don't know,

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:09.660
probably 1900 or something.

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:11.970
And it basically
says, in that case--

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:13.620
and it's completely reasonable.

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:15.330
It says, if lambda
is 1 and you want

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:17.520
to minimize say the
total number of errors,

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:21.330
you would say,
well, what I would

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:24.630
do is I will choose
the distribution which

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:25.717
had higher likelihood.

00:05:25.717 --> 00:05:27.300
And we'll see that
in another context.

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:29.883
Or we've already seen it in the
context of maximum likelihood,

00:05:29.883 --> 00:05:32.170
so that's an example of that.

00:05:32.170 --> 00:05:34.320
And the lambda allows
you to put weights

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:36.267
on the two different
types of errors, which

00:05:36.267 --> 00:05:38.100
I think are called type
I and type II error,

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:40.110
but I can never
remember which is which

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:42.420
and just look it up every
time I need to know.

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:44.550
Actually, usually I
try to avoid situations

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:47.440
where people are saying things
like type I and type II error.

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:49.950
So I usually walk out of the
room, but if I am forced to,

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:52.410
I will just look it up
to figure out what it is.

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:54.510
OK.

00:05:54.510 --> 00:05:58.530
Now what's interesting is even
at this super-simple problem

00:05:58.530 --> 00:06:00.930
you can get into weird
things-- very interesting stuff

00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:01.510
like this.

00:06:01.510 --> 00:06:03.330
Suppose you say,
I want to minimize

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:06.390
the maximum of my false
probability and my--

00:06:06.390 --> 00:06:09.990
false-positive and
false-negative errors.

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:11.880
That will get you
something that's

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:13.920
pretty [INAUDIBLE]
optimal because max is

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:16.920
a monotone function of the two.

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:20.040
It's a monotone mapping from
the two criteria into one,

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:22.290
so that is a scalarization.

00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:24.180
It's not a linear
scalarization, which

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:29.040
is what's normally employed,
but it's a scalarization.

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:34.890
And turns out that's an LP,
and the solution is usually not

00:06:34.890 --> 00:06:36.300
deterministic.

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:38.890
So here's an example.

00:06:38.890 --> 00:06:40.990
So here we go.

00:06:40.990 --> 00:06:42.210
Let's see.

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:44.820
Here's the trade off
curve of false-positive

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:45.930
versus false-negative.

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:47.910
That's this thing,
this curve here.

00:06:47.910 --> 00:06:52.698
And oh yeah, somebody
explain the point at the top.

00:06:52.698 --> 00:06:54.240
First of all, let's
talk about what's

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:57.760
really good about that detector.

00:06:57.760 --> 00:06:58.990
What's the probability?

00:06:58.990 --> 00:07:01.620
If we use the detector
up there at 1,

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:04.690
this point right
here, this one--

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:06.648
first, let's say the
good part about it.

00:07:06.648 --> 00:07:09.190
What's the good-- what is the
probability of a false-positive

00:07:09.190 --> 00:07:10.180
for that detector?

00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:11.370
0.

00:07:11.370 --> 00:07:13.740
0, right, OK, fine.

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:16.630
By the way, what does
that detector do?

00:07:16.630 --> 00:07:20.985
It just returns negative every
time, so it's kind of silly.

00:07:20.985 --> 00:07:22.360
But the good news
is it does have

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:24.235
a very low false-positive rate.

00:07:28.540 --> 00:07:31.600
And that's-- anyway, each
of these correspond--

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:37.780
these correspond to likelihood
ratios between the entries

00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:39.190
there.

00:07:39.190 --> 00:07:42.370
What is your-- in
fact, you can even

00:07:42.370 --> 00:07:44.737
take lambda and
put lambda around

00:07:44.737 --> 00:07:45.820
here and ask what happens.

00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:47.927
If you have a certain
lambda like that,

00:07:47.927 --> 00:07:49.510
the optimal point
you're going to find

00:07:49.510 --> 00:07:51.595
is going to be here
or here or here.

00:07:54.370 --> 00:07:57.190
The minimax one is one-- you
can figure out what that is.

00:07:57.190 --> 00:08:01.420
This is the x equals y line,
and the minimax one you find

00:08:01.420 --> 00:08:03.100
by simply taking
this rectangle--

00:08:03.100 --> 00:08:06.010
or square if this was
done correctly with axes,

00:08:06.010 --> 00:08:07.510
which it was not--

00:08:07.510 --> 00:08:10.610
and you simply grow it
until it touches this curve.

00:08:10.610 --> 00:08:12.050
And that's going
to be right here.

00:08:12.050 --> 00:08:15.130
And so it turns out if you
wanted to minimize the maximum

00:08:15.130 --> 00:08:20.780
of your false-positive and your
false-negative, you would do--

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:22.430
for number one, you'd have a--

00:08:22.430 --> 00:08:25.640
you'd have a non-deterministic
detector, which is weird

00:08:25.640 --> 00:08:29.180
because you'd say, oh, the
outcome was, I don't know,

00:08:29.180 --> 00:08:30.560
let's say 2.

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:33.650
And you'd go, oh, yeah, OK,
well, then that's definitely P.

00:08:33.650 --> 00:08:36.692
And then you'd-- then you'd come
back later and say the outcome

00:08:36.692 --> 00:08:38.150
was 2, and you'd
say, I don't know.

00:08:38.150 --> 00:08:39.275
Let me check just a minute.

00:08:39.275 --> 00:08:40.130
Oh, it's q.

00:08:40.130 --> 00:08:41.330
Everybody got this?

00:08:41.330 --> 00:08:43.460
So it's a little
bit odd that you

00:08:43.460 --> 00:08:45.020
would have a
randomized detector,

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:47.125
but this comes up in
a bunch of things.

00:08:47.125 --> 00:08:48.500
By the way, a
randomized detector

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:52.460
you can think of as a
relaxation of the original--

00:08:52.460 --> 00:08:55.250
the original sensible
problem is to say, no.

00:08:55.250 --> 00:09:00.320
If there's an outcome, you have
to commit to one of these two

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:01.160
distributions.

00:09:01.160 --> 00:09:04.280
You can't do something weird
and have an inconsistent weird

00:09:04.280 --> 00:09:07.370
probabilistic thing.

00:09:07.370 --> 00:09:08.750
OK, so all right.

00:09:08.750 --> 00:09:12.080
So that's very baby example.

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:15.110
OK, last topic in
this section is

00:09:15.110 --> 00:09:17.090
going to be experiment design.

00:09:17.090 --> 00:09:21.780
This is actually super
interesting, also super useful.

00:09:21.780 --> 00:09:24.560
So the setting is we have a
bunch of linear measurements

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:27.350
here, so we have
linear measurements,

00:09:27.350 --> 00:09:32.930
and ai can think of as
the measurement vector.

00:09:32.930 --> 00:09:36.410
wi-- we're going
to normalize those

00:09:36.410 --> 00:09:41.060
to have all the same standard
deviation, which is 1.

00:09:41.060 --> 00:09:44.000
And so what it means,
roughly speaking, the size

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:46.430
of a is something like--

00:09:46.430 --> 00:09:51.200
and this is dialect, but
signal to noise ratio

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:54.470
because if a is
gigantic, then it

00:09:54.470 --> 00:09:58.160
says that the ai transpose
x tells you a lot,

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:02.180
and then I add this noise that's
plus minus 1, who cares, right?

00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:06.000
If ai is small, it means that's
a pretty crappy measurement,

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:10.020
so that's the idea there.

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:12.410
Now, if I actually
take-- if I get

00:10:12.410 --> 00:10:15.450
a bunch of these measurements,
these yi's, and I

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:19.050
ask someone to guess x
and the maximum likelihood

00:10:19.050 --> 00:10:20.640
is just nothing
but least squares,

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:23.170
the estimate is just
the least squares.

00:10:23.170 --> 00:10:24.390
So you just get this.

00:10:24.390 --> 00:10:27.960
It's the sum of aiai
transpose-- that's this thing--

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:30.330
inverse times this.

00:10:30.330 --> 00:10:34.710
And the error, which is x
hat minus x, has 0 mean,

00:10:34.710 --> 00:10:36.780
so that's an unbiased--

00:10:36.780 --> 00:10:38.970
that's an unbiased estimate.

00:10:38.970 --> 00:10:43.380
And it has a covariance, which
is given by this matrix here.

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:46.200
And if you wanted to work out a
confidence ellipsoid for that,

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:48.610
again, assuming because
these are all Gaussian--

00:10:48.610 --> 00:10:51.000
this is Gaussian--

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:52.000
it would look like this.

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:53.417
It would say it's
a set of x which

00:10:53.417 --> 00:10:55.470
are centered at
your estimate x hat,

00:10:55.470 --> 00:11:01.780
and the shape is given
by this matrix here.

00:11:01.780 --> 00:11:02.908
And so for example--

00:11:02.908 --> 00:11:03.450
I don't know.

00:11:03.450 --> 00:11:06.900
So that would give you-- and
beta is going to be determined

00:11:06.900 --> 00:11:07.870
by--

00:11:07.870 --> 00:11:10.650
actually, it would be something
like the inverse distribution

00:11:10.650 --> 00:11:13.170
of a chi squared random
variable with some number

00:11:13.170 --> 00:11:14.940
with the appropriate
number of degrees.

00:11:14.940 --> 00:11:17.400
It doesn't matter,
though, because that's

00:11:17.400 --> 00:11:19.860
how you would get beta,
and that would say,

00:11:19.860 --> 00:11:21.750
thank you for the measurements.

00:11:21.750 --> 00:11:26.250
This tells you with 90%
probability you're in this--

00:11:26.250 --> 00:11:28.290
you're in this ellipsoid.

00:11:28.290 --> 00:11:32.850
And if I say 99% thing, then
beta just gets bigger here,

00:11:32.850 --> 00:11:34.410
so that's all that happens.

00:11:34.410 --> 00:11:35.930
OK.

00:11:35.930 --> 00:11:39.600
So experiment design
is this problem,

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:43.490
so I have a bunch of
candidates, v1 through vp.

00:11:43.490 --> 00:11:46.430
You should think of these
as possible measurements

00:11:46.430 --> 00:11:52.700
or experiments you can make,
so that's what they are.

00:11:52.700 --> 00:11:58.070
And then I want
to choose m ai's,

00:11:58.070 --> 00:12:03.350
which are among this palette
of possible experiments

00:12:03.350 --> 00:12:04.550
I can carry out.

00:12:04.550 --> 00:12:06.950
So in other words, I
have a bunch of sensors,

00:12:06.950 --> 00:12:12.440
and what I can do
is you'd say, pick

00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:14.270
which sensors you should use.

00:12:14.270 --> 00:12:16.850
In fact, even pick
the sequence which

00:12:16.850 --> 00:12:20.810
you should use to actually
get the best estimate.

00:12:20.810 --> 00:12:22.550
The best estimate
is a bit tricky

00:12:22.550 --> 00:12:24.470
because we're going
to measure everything

00:12:24.470 --> 00:12:28.820
in terms of this
ellipsoid, and we want--

00:12:28.820 --> 00:12:32.090
sorry, this positive definite
matrix, and what we really want

00:12:32.090 --> 00:12:34.830
is this positive definite matrix
to be small, which, by the way,

00:12:34.830 --> 00:12:36.390
is the same as saying that the--

00:12:36.390 --> 00:12:36.990
sorry.

00:12:36.990 --> 00:12:38.640
Yeah, we want this
to be small, which

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:41.850
is saying that the confidence
ellipsoid is small,

00:12:41.850 --> 00:12:42.780
so that's what you do.

00:12:42.780 --> 00:12:45.510
So in other words, now the
problem with that is this

00:12:45.510 --> 00:12:49.240
is exactly an ordering, the
positive semidefinite matrices,

00:12:49.240 --> 00:12:51.120
which is not linear.

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:54.180
So it's completely reasonable if
you came up with an experiment

00:12:54.180 --> 00:12:58.410
design and you came up with
one, we looked at your Es,

00:12:58.410 --> 00:13:02.380
like E1 and E2, and they
were not comparable.

00:13:02.380 --> 00:13:04.195
So in some directions
you're better,

00:13:04.195 --> 00:13:05.820
and in other directions,
you're better.

00:13:05.820 --> 00:13:08.220
That can and does happen.

00:13:08.220 --> 00:13:09.750
Everybody got it?

00:13:09.750 --> 00:13:12.300
So we're going to
have to scalarize

00:13:12.300 --> 00:13:15.367
the idea of a positive
definite matrix being small,

00:13:15.367 --> 00:13:17.200
and there's lots of
different ways to do it.

00:13:17.200 --> 00:13:19.620
And each one is going to
lead to a certain named

00:13:19.620 --> 00:13:21.700
type of experiment design.

00:13:21.700 --> 00:13:25.020
So let me discuss a
couple of things quickly.

00:13:25.020 --> 00:13:29.665
Earlier I said that the size
of A is something like--

00:13:29.665 --> 00:13:30.540
let's say the 2-norm.

00:13:30.540 --> 00:13:33.000
That's something like a
signal-to-noise ratio.

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:35.430
If ai is gigantic,
that's really good.

00:13:35.430 --> 00:13:37.230
That's a good measurement.

00:13:37.230 --> 00:13:39.290
So let me ask a question here.

00:13:39.290 --> 00:13:41.232
I have a proposal.

00:13:41.232 --> 00:13:42.690
Here's my experiment
design method.

00:13:42.690 --> 00:13:43.980
Ready?

00:13:43.980 --> 00:13:48.087
I will find the largest
vi in magnitude.

00:13:48.087 --> 00:13:49.920
And someone says, why
are you choosing that?

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:53.387
And you go, of the
sensors or the experiments

00:13:53.387 --> 00:13:55.470
I've been offered, that's
the one with the highest

00:13:55.470 --> 00:13:56.730
signal-to-noise ratio.

00:13:56.730 --> 00:13:58.110
Makes sense?

00:13:58.110 --> 00:13:58.710
OK.

00:13:58.710 --> 00:14:01.320
And then they'd say, ready
for my experiment design?

00:14:01.320 --> 00:14:03.780
Just do that.

00:14:03.780 --> 00:14:06.388
suppose v7 is the largest
in magnitude of these.

00:14:06.388 --> 00:14:08.430
Then you just do that the
whole way, all the way.

00:14:08.430 --> 00:14:10.138
And someone says, why
are you doing that?

00:14:10.138 --> 00:14:12.870
And you go, why would
I use anything other

00:14:12.870 --> 00:14:17.400
than the experiment that has the
highest signal-to-noise ratio?

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:21.600
Everybody got that?

00:14:21.600 --> 00:14:23.850
Well, OK, you're
probably not going

00:14:23.850 --> 00:14:25.740
to fool for it, but
I'm actually pretty

00:14:25.740 --> 00:14:26.940
good at making up a good--

00:14:26.940 --> 00:14:30.420
I could tell stories around that
and all sorts of other stuff.

00:14:30.420 --> 00:14:33.150
I bet I could get some
people to go for that.

00:14:33.150 --> 00:14:33.855
Any comments?

00:14:36.954 --> 00:14:41.000
You want the worst situation,
not the best situation

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:42.780
[INAUDIBLE].

00:14:42.780 --> 00:14:46.350
Well, no, I just
told you my policy.

00:14:46.350 --> 00:14:49.620
My policy is I'm going to
take v7 over here because it's

00:14:49.620 --> 00:14:52.470
the one with the
largest magnitude,

00:14:52.470 --> 00:14:55.650
and then I'm just going to-- all
the ai's are going to be that.

00:14:55.650 --> 00:14:59.910
You have a suggestion,
any comment on it?

00:14:59.910 --> 00:15:03.210
Would there be some
bias in one direction?

00:15:03.210 --> 00:15:04.550
No.

00:15:04.550 --> 00:15:06.390
All of these are
kind of unbiased.

00:15:06.390 --> 00:15:07.860
Yeah.

00:15:07.860 --> 00:15:10.860
Let me ask one question.

00:15:10.860 --> 00:15:13.050
What is-- let's talk
about this matrix

00:15:13.050 --> 00:15:14.745
if all the ai's were the same.

00:15:17.730 --> 00:15:19.380
What's that matrix look like?

00:15:22.610 --> 00:15:25.730
What is it?

00:15:25.730 --> 00:15:27.410
v7 squared identity.

00:15:27.410 --> 00:15:28.700
It's what?

00:15:28.700 --> 00:15:32.000
Whatever parameter of v7,
and then v7 squared and doing

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:33.810
identity matrix.

00:15:33.810 --> 00:15:35.630
I don't see an identity matrix.

00:15:35.630 --> 00:15:37.310
What's that?

00:15:37.310 --> 00:15:39.950
Yeah, so just this
thing would become sum

00:15:39.950 --> 00:15:44.120
from i equals 1 to m v7v7
transpose, otherwise known

00:15:44.120 --> 00:15:48.870
as m times v7v7 transpose.

00:15:48.870 --> 00:15:53.520
Anybody got a comment about
the matrix v7v7 transpose?

00:15:53.520 --> 00:15:54.060
It's what?

00:15:54.060 --> 00:15:55.720
It's rank one, right?

00:15:55.720 --> 00:15:56.220
OK.

00:15:56.220 --> 00:15:59.285
So if I just always use
the experiment with the--

00:15:59.285 --> 00:16:01.410
if I always make a measurement
which is the highest

00:16:01.410 --> 00:16:07.210
signal-to-noise ratio, then
we got a kind of a problem

00:16:07.210 --> 00:16:10.660
here because this matrix
is not invertible.

00:16:10.660 --> 00:16:12.370
It's singular.

00:16:12.370 --> 00:16:15.700
And so we could talk about
e inverse if you like.

00:16:15.700 --> 00:16:16.660
It's kind of silly.

00:16:16.660 --> 00:16:18.100
I can tell you what it would be.

00:16:18.100 --> 00:16:19.183
It's our error covariance.

00:16:19.183 --> 00:16:22.810
It'd be really good
in the direction v7

00:16:22.810 --> 00:16:24.100
because I blew my--

00:16:24.100 --> 00:16:27.490
every experiment I ever did
was the same experiment.

00:16:27.490 --> 00:16:31.660
But in n minus 1
dimensions-- yeah, OK.

00:16:31.660 --> 00:16:34.870
In n minus 1 dimensions, I
got no information of any kind

00:16:34.870 --> 00:16:37.787
whatsoever, and so my confidence
ellipsoid is, like, infinite

00:16:37.787 --> 00:16:38.620
in those directions.

00:16:38.620 --> 00:16:42.010
Everybody-- makes sense?

00:16:42.010 --> 00:16:42.700
Yep.

00:16:42.700 --> 00:16:43.270
OK.

00:16:43.270 --> 00:16:43.900
So that's it.

00:16:43.900 --> 00:16:45.790
OK.

00:16:45.790 --> 00:16:47.380
All right.

00:16:47.380 --> 00:16:50.110
So the first thing to--

00:16:50.110 --> 00:16:53.200
there's another thing
we can observe here.

00:16:53.200 --> 00:16:57.220
Here we don't have to
choose the sequence

00:16:57.220 --> 00:17:01.060
because the outcome doesn't
depend on the sequencing.

00:17:01.060 --> 00:17:04.089
So in other words, then
it says no difference

00:17:04.089 --> 00:17:06.369
if someone says, please
do the following,

00:17:06.369 --> 00:17:11.260
use v1 four times then v3,
and then here's the trick,

00:17:11.260 --> 00:17:14.440
switch back to v1 four
more times and then v4.

00:17:14.440 --> 00:17:16.329
Everybody got what I just said?

00:17:16.329 --> 00:17:18.160
So it turns out, doesn't matter.

00:17:18.160 --> 00:17:21.460
If you look at our objective
function here, it's symmetric.

00:17:21.460 --> 00:17:23.500
You can permute them
any way you like.

00:17:23.500 --> 00:17:27.069
By the way, this is
completely false,

00:17:27.069 --> 00:17:31.360
except in this incredibly
simple Gaussian setup.

00:17:31.360 --> 00:17:34.630
In other cases, you want to
do design of experiments.

00:17:34.630 --> 00:17:37.360
It's a whole sequence,
and it depends

00:17:37.360 --> 00:17:40.780
on all-- it depends on what
you've observed so far.

00:17:40.780 --> 00:17:42.910
In other words, the best--

00:17:42.910 --> 00:17:46.090
if we're flying a drone
and someone says, which--

00:17:46.090 --> 00:17:51.770
we have an energy budget, which
sensor are we going to use,

00:17:51.770 --> 00:17:54.240
it depends on all
sorts of stuff.

00:17:54.240 --> 00:17:57.770
But in this Gaussian model,
actually, it doesn't.

00:17:57.770 --> 00:17:59.330
So it really only depends--

00:17:59.330 --> 00:18:01.640
I only have to tell
you how many of my--

00:18:01.640 --> 00:18:03.110
my total budget
is m experiments.

00:18:03.110 --> 00:18:06.410
I just have to divide
m up across the palette

00:18:06.410 --> 00:18:09.470
of potential experiments
that I've been offered.

00:18:09.470 --> 00:18:12.020
So if I had 22, I
could say, I want

00:18:12.020 --> 00:18:14.510
to do four of these, seven
of those, three of these,

00:18:14.510 --> 00:18:17.330
and blah, blah, blah, and my
numbers better add up to 22.

00:18:17.330 --> 00:18:18.620
That's all we have to do.

00:18:18.620 --> 00:18:20.180
OK.

00:18:20.180 --> 00:18:22.830
So we'll write it this way.

00:18:22.830 --> 00:18:25.400
And here we have
an integer problem

00:18:25.400 --> 00:18:27.530
because the m's are integers.

00:18:27.530 --> 00:18:30.560
Actually, here mk is
the number of times

00:18:30.560 --> 00:18:35.110
I'm going to use
experiment k or sensor k.

00:18:35.110 --> 00:18:36.740
That's what it is.

00:18:36.740 --> 00:18:39.710
And then this thing
just looks like this.

00:18:39.710 --> 00:18:43.700
OK, so this is all cool.

00:18:43.700 --> 00:18:44.480
Let's see.

00:18:44.480 --> 00:18:47.780
This is even--
this is actually--

00:18:47.780 --> 00:18:51.102
if m were-- if m were not an
integer but a real number,

00:18:51.102 --> 00:18:53.060
positive real number,
this thing would actually

00:18:53.060 --> 00:19:01.740
be k convex where k is the PSD
cone, so this would be cool.

00:19:01.740 --> 00:19:04.050
But it's not. m is an integer.

00:19:04.050 --> 00:19:07.080
And now-- by the way, this comes
up in lots and lots of settings

00:19:07.080 --> 00:19:09.390
where there's an integer
or something like that,

00:19:09.390 --> 00:19:11.160
and then so you do--

00:19:11.160 --> 00:19:15.180
it's kind of one of the
themes is you do relaxation.

00:19:15.180 --> 00:19:18.600
So the relaxation
is, you know what,

00:19:18.600 --> 00:19:23.700
I divide the m's by capital M.
So instead of working with m1,

00:19:23.700 --> 00:19:26.010
I'm going to work with
m1 divided by capital M.

00:19:26.010 --> 00:19:28.282
And then you're saying
what is the fraction

00:19:28.282 --> 00:19:30.240
of the total number of
experiments you're going

00:19:30.240 --> 00:19:32.170
to do that are going to use--

00:19:32.170 --> 00:19:34.650
in fact, m1 over M is the--
what is the fraction when

00:19:34.650 --> 00:19:37.230
you use experiment number one?

00:19:37.230 --> 00:19:38.470
Make sense?

00:19:38.470 --> 00:19:38.970
OK.

00:19:38.970 --> 00:19:44.640
So now, if I do that, we
still have the constraint

00:19:44.640 --> 00:19:49.340
that those fractions are
integer multiples of 1/m.

00:19:49.340 --> 00:19:53.060
So now what we're going to do
is we're going to relax it,

00:19:53.060 --> 00:19:56.120
so we're going to simply
ignore that last constraint.

00:19:56.120 --> 00:19:59.330
That's a typical relaxation.

00:19:59.330 --> 00:20:01.580
Relaxation in general
just means you

00:20:01.580 --> 00:20:04.650
made the feasible set bigger.

00:20:04.650 --> 00:20:09.500
That's all it means,
and so we do that.

00:20:09.500 --> 00:20:12.590
Now this-- this is actually--
it's kind of cool if--

00:20:12.590 --> 00:20:15.200
so we're going to
relax it like this,

00:20:15.200 --> 00:20:17.970
and then there's all sorts of
things you can do with this.

00:20:17.970 --> 00:20:20.708
So we'll talk about solving
it, but what's going to come up

00:20:20.708 --> 00:20:22.250
is you say, actually,
what I'm really

00:20:22.250 --> 00:20:24.800
going to do is I'm
going to choose

00:20:24.800 --> 00:20:29.450
for you the fractions of
each of the experiment types

00:20:29.450 --> 00:20:33.695
that you should use,
whatever your budget is.

00:20:33.695 --> 00:20:36.320
Maybe it's not going to fit into
the integers, but if m is big.

00:20:36.320 --> 00:20:38.360
I don't think this is a problem.

00:20:38.360 --> 00:20:40.280
So and then I'll give
you lots of hints

00:20:40.280 --> 00:20:43.460
about what you could do.

00:20:43.460 --> 00:20:44.690
What you can do--

00:20:44.690 --> 00:20:46.190
first, you go back
to the person who

00:20:46.190 --> 00:20:49.188
said, do the experiment design,
and you'd say, yeah, OK,

00:20:49.188 --> 00:20:49.980
what's your budget?

00:20:49.980 --> 00:20:52.710
And they'd say, 57, and they
go, great, you should do--

00:20:52.710 --> 00:20:57.150
you should use experiment
number two 6.45 times.

00:20:57.150 --> 00:21:00.090
And they would go like,
what does that mean?

00:21:00.090 --> 00:21:00.810
It means nothing.

00:21:00.810 --> 00:21:04.235
I can't use-- every
time I'm allowed

00:21:04.235 --> 00:21:05.610
to choose one of
the experiments,

00:21:05.610 --> 00:21:07.500
and I can't do it 6.45 times.

00:21:07.500 --> 00:21:08.500
Everybody see that?

00:21:08.500 --> 00:21:09.000
OK.

00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:11.010
At this point-- and this
is just very practical

00:21:11.010 --> 00:21:14.580
advice for all of
you when you become

00:21:14.580 --> 00:21:18.360
part of the 364A diaspora.

00:21:18.360 --> 00:21:20.970
Here's what you're going to--
the first thing you do is you

00:21:20.970 --> 00:21:24.960
try to convince the person that
your relaxation is actually

00:21:24.960 --> 00:21:29.010
much more sophisticated, and
you'd say, oh, yeah, no, look--

00:21:29.010 --> 00:21:30.600
don't tell me
about six or seven.

00:21:30.600 --> 00:21:31.800
I don't care about that.

00:21:31.800 --> 00:21:33.310
This is much more sophisticated.

00:21:33.310 --> 00:21:36.210
You can drop things you can make
reference to quantum mechanics.

00:21:36.210 --> 00:21:39.120
That sometimes helps
because you'd say,

00:21:39.120 --> 00:21:43.117
it's not like you just choose
an experiment each time.

00:21:43.117 --> 00:21:45.450
Anyway, you could say this
is really like a probability.

00:21:45.450 --> 00:21:47.640
These are-- in fact, the lambdas
are going to be probabilities.

00:21:47.640 --> 00:21:49.473
And then it says, here's
what you should do.

00:21:49.473 --> 00:21:52.540
Every time you should roll the
dice with probability lambda,

00:21:52.540 --> 00:21:56.160
and if 13 comes up,
congratulations.

00:21:56.160 --> 00:21:57.900
You should do experiment 13.

00:21:57.900 --> 00:21:59.400
Everybody following
what I'm saying?

00:21:59.400 --> 00:22:02.400
Anyway, some people
actually fall for this.

00:22:02.400 --> 00:22:04.020
It's actually kind of amazing.

00:22:04.020 --> 00:22:07.825
And you mentioned-- and you
mumble about quantum mechanics

00:22:07.825 --> 00:22:08.700
and things like that.

00:22:08.700 --> 00:22:09.908
It's much more sophisticated.

00:22:09.908 --> 00:22:11.135
It's a probabilistic thing.

00:22:11.135 --> 00:22:12.510
And you can hint
at other things.

00:22:12.510 --> 00:22:14.760
Like, hey, do you
remember matrix games?

00:22:14.760 --> 00:22:18.010
Do you remember how there's
generally not an equilibrium

00:22:18.010 --> 00:22:20.940
and so you have to have
a-- what do you call it?

00:22:20.940 --> 00:22:24.090
What's it called--
a randomized policy?

00:22:24.090 --> 00:22:28.380
And they would go, yeah, and you
go, well, it's just like that.

00:22:28.380 --> 00:22:31.050
Anyway, that's fine.

00:22:31.050 --> 00:22:35.020
But supposing, in fact,
you really had to do--

00:22:35.020 --> 00:22:38.550
someone said, no, dude,
the drone is flying,

00:22:38.550 --> 00:22:41.590
and you have a budget
of 120 experiments,

00:22:41.590 --> 00:22:44.550
so I need integers.

00:22:44.550 --> 00:22:47.235
So someone tell me,
what would you do?

00:22:52.160 --> 00:22:52.940
What would you do?

00:22:52.940 --> 00:22:54.410
Threshold thing?

00:22:54.410 --> 00:22:55.430
Threshold-- yeah.

00:22:55.430 --> 00:22:56.930
Do you mean rounding?

00:22:56.930 --> 00:22:57.860
Yeah, yeah.

00:22:57.860 --> 00:22:58.980
Yeah, here's what you do.

00:22:58.980 --> 00:22:59.980
You'd find your lambdas.

00:22:59.980 --> 00:23:02.240
They're going to be--
it's going to be sort of--

00:23:02.240 --> 00:23:04.460
it's a continuous
probability that you

00:23:04.460 --> 00:23:06.350
choose experiment or fraction.

00:23:06.350 --> 00:23:07.888
And you just around them.

00:23:07.888 --> 00:23:09.680
By the way, if you
around them and then you

00:23:09.680 --> 00:23:12.620
add up the rounded things
and they come up to 121,

00:23:12.620 --> 00:23:14.600
what do you do then?

00:23:14.600 --> 00:23:16.568
Just reduce one.

00:23:16.568 --> 00:23:17.360
That's what you do.

00:23:17.360 --> 00:23:18.290
Everybody following this?

00:23:18.290 --> 00:23:19.580
And I'm not kidding with this.

00:23:19.580 --> 00:23:21.410
And you say, that's
my experiment design.

00:23:21.410 --> 00:23:23.630
And they would say, awesome.

00:23:23.630 --> 00:23:27.710
And they would say, is
that actually the one that

00:23:27.710 --> 00:23:30.415
is going to minimize the--

00:23:30.415 --> 00:23:31.790
is that going to
give me the-- is

00:23:31.790 --> 00:23:33.040
that going to be the best one?

00:23:33.040 --> 00:23:37.550
Is it going to minimize
this confidence ellipsoid?

00:23:37.550 --> 00:23:41.400
What's the honest
answer to that?

00:23:41.400 --> 00:23:42.920
No.

00:23:42.920 --> 00:23:44.750
Yeah, that probably would work.

00:23:44.750 --> 00:23:45.530
That's fair.

00:23:45.530 --> 00:23:48.833
That's one way to say no,
but yeah, so you'd say,

00:23:48.833 --> 00:23:51.500
does it solve-- they'd say, does
it actually solve this problem?

00:23:51.500 --> 00:23:54.260
And you'd say, I
don't really know.

00:23:54.260 --> 00:23:56.360
But what can you--
what can you do

00:23:56.360 --> 00:23:58.040
to give some
evidence that you're

00:23:58.040 --> 00:23:59.375
rounded one is not so bad?

00:24:05.730 --> 00:24:07.220
You do the dual problem?

00:24:07.220 --> 00:24:07.860
Yeah.

00:24:07.860 --> 00:24:10.890
You don't even need the dual
because this was a relaxation,

00:24:10.890 --> 00:24:13.670
so what you do is-- well,
wait until-- this will make

00:24:13.670 --> 00:24:15.620
more sense when we scalarize.

00:24:15.620 --> 00:24:17.870
So let's say, for
example, what happens

00:24:17.870 --> 00:24:20.960
is you end up with the volume
of the confidence ellipsoid,

00:24:20.960 --> 00:24:22.980
and someone says, does
that choice actually

00:24:22.980 --> 00:24:24.980
minimize the volume of
the confidence ellipsoid?

00:24:24.980 --> 00:24:28.520
And you go-- actually, your
answer was perfect, probably.

00:24:28.520 --> 00:24:30.995
You'd say, it's very close,
and they'd say, well,

00:24:30.995 --> 00:24:31.870
how do you know that?

00:24:31.870 --> 00:24:34.795
You'd go, well, because
when I remove the constraint

00:24:34.795 --> 00:24:37.170
that these things are integers
and I solved this problem,

00:24:37.170 --> 00:24:40.580
I found that the log
of the volume was 12.6.

00:24:40.580 --> 00:24:43.580
And you'd say, nobody
could do better than that.

00:24:43.580 --> 00:24:45.258
That's if we ignore
the silly constraint

00:24:45.258 --> 00:24:46.550
that these things are integers.

00:24:46.550 --> 00:24:50.570
And you'd say, my
volume, my log volume,

00:24:50.570 --> 00:24:53.000
was 12.7 when I rounded.

00:24:53.000 --> 00:24:54.050
Everybody got that?

00:24:54.050 --> 00:24:56.660
Then it's done.

00:24:56.660 --> 00:24:58.820
Then you're just done
because you just say,

00:24:58.820 --> 00:25:00.855
I can show nobody
can have a volume--

00:25:00.855 --> 00:25:01.730
did I get that right?

00:25:01.730 --> 00:25:02.522
I did get it right.

00:25:02.522 --> 00:25:04.820
No one can have a
volume less than 12.6.

00:25:04.820 --> 00:25:06.920
That's something you can--

00:25:06.920 --> 00:25:10.280
but we got one that
has log volume 12.7.

00:25:10.280 --> 00:25:11.690
Makes sense?

00:25:11.690 --> 00:25:12.830
OK.

00:25:12.830 --> 00:25:16.033
All right, so this is
relax experiment design.

00:25:16.033 --> 00:25:18.200
Actually, we will get to
the dual of this very soon.

00:25:18.200 --> 00:25:19.820
Yes?

00:25:19.820 --> 00:25:21.710
--assumption of m
must be much bigger

00:25:21.710 --> 00:25:23.480
than the number of experiments.

00:25:23.480 --> 00:25:24.110
Sorry, where?

00:25:24.110 --> 00:25:24.610
Here?

00:25:24.610 --> 00:25:26.330
m much bigger than p.

00:25:26.330 --> 00:25:27.800
The assumption assumed--

00:25:27.800 --> 00:25:29.690
Why is--

00:25:29.690 --> 00:25:32.900
The relax experiment design
assumed m much bigger than p

00:25:32.900 --> 00:25:36.428
and then using lambda k
equal to m [INAUDIBLE]..

00:25:36.428 --> 00:25:37.220
You mean over here?

00:25:37.220 --> 00:25:37.910
Yeah.

00:25:37.910 --> 00:25:40.950
Why is that there?

00:25:40.950 --> 00:25:42.960
Yeah, let's see why.

00:25:42.960 --> 00:25:48.420
Oh, the idea is I wanted these
numbers to be reasonably--

00:25:48.420 --> 00:25:50.340
to be not too grainy.

00:25:50.340 --> 00:25:52.410
If I round it, I want to
be able to get something

00:25:52.410 --> 00:25:54.310
that's close to an integer.

00:25:54.310 --> 00:25:56.820
You don't even need
this, the first one.

00:25:56.820 --> 00:25:59.880
That doesn't matter.

00:25:59.880 --> 00:26:04.410
Now, when you get down to
it-- well, OK, let's try this.

00:26:04.410 --> 00:26:10.577
What if I said, you have
three experiments, period?

00:26:10.577 --> 00:26:11.660
Can we solve that problem?

00:26:15.140 --> 00:26:16.940
It depends how big
whatever it is,

00:26:16.940 --> 00:26:19.580
p is, right? p is
the number of--

00:26:19.580 --> 00:26:21.950
p is the number
of options, right?

00:26:21.950 --> 00:26:23.780
Let's suppose p is
20 or something,

00:26:23.780 --> 00:26:26.660
and I tell you, no, you
have three experiments.

00:26:26.660 --> 00:26:28.540
Tell me what to do.

00:26:28.540 --> 00:26:30.230
Can we solve that?

00:26:30.230 --> 00:26:31.480
Just try all the combinations.

00:26:31.480 --> 00:26:32.320
Yeah, try all.

00:26:32.320 --> 00:26:33.250
It's like p cubed.

00:26:33.250 --> 00:26:35.380
You just do-- you
try all combinations

00:26:35.380 --> 00:26:36.880
or whatever-- it's
not even p cubed.

00:26:36.880 --> 00:26:40.300
It's a lot less than that,
p cubed over 6 or something

00:26:40.300 --> 00:26:41.050
like that.

00:26:41.050 --> 00:26:43.690
You try all of them,
and you find out.

00:26:43.690 --> 00:26:46.717
So for small ones, we can
actually solve the actual--

00:26:46.717 --> 00:26:48.550
and that's actually
when you get into things

00:26:48.550 --> 00:26:49.790
like design of experiments.

00:26:49.790 --> 00:26:53.980
These are grainy, and they're
kind of combinatorial.

00:26:53.980 --> 00:26:55.240
And these are tricky.

00:26:55.240 --> 00:26:56.800
Yeah?

00:26:56.800 --> 00:27:00.610
Perhaps I didn't understand
what you meant before about E

00:27:00.610 --> 00:27:03.430
not being invertible,
but doesn't this not

00:27:03.430 --> 00:27:05.510
require that E is invertible?

00:27:05.510 --> 00:27:06.010
Yeah.

00:27:06.010 --> 00:27:09.400
Because you can make a solution
where m1 is equal to M?

00:27:09.400 --> 00:27:10.030
Yeah.

00:27:10.030 --> 00:27:11.890
Yeah.

00:27:11.890 --> 00:27:13.900
So you're going to
have a problem, though.

00:27:13.900 --> 00:27:16.000
Let me show you
what the problem is,

00:27:16.000 --> 00:27:18.280
that when this is
not invertible,

00:27:18.280 --> 00:27:21.640
we're going to interpret
E as being basically

00:27:21.640 --> 00:27:26.070
infinite in those
directions, and we've

00:27:26.070 --> 00:27:27.210
asked you to minimize it.

00:27:27.210 --> 00:27:28.590
By the way, when
we scalarize it,

00:27:28.590 --> 00:27:29.730
we're going to be
cool because we're

00:27:29.730 --> 00:27:31.605
going to have a log of
determinant or a trace

00:27:31.605 --> 00:27:32.770
and all that kind of stuff.

00:27:32.770 --> 00:27:34.590
And the problem
is then you'd say,

00:27:34.590 --> 00:27:37.560
if you do that, you're going
to come up with plus infinity.

00:27:37.560 --> 00:27:40.500
Yeah, and so we'll get to that
in a minute, yeah, but that's--

00:27:40.500 --> 00:27:43.464
It's the fact that
your objective function

00:27:43.464 --> 00:27:46.040
or your-- yeah, your objective
function is making it

00:27:46.040 --> 00:27:47.160
undesirable for it to be--

00:27:47.160 --> 00:27:47.660
Yes.

00:27:47.660 --> 00:27:50.910
In fact, in this case,
infinitely undesirable.

00:27:50.910 --> 00:27:53.930
So what we'll do is we'll
say that if this thing is

00:27:53.930 --> 00:27:57.338
invertible, we'll say then
E is infinitely undesirable.

00:27:57.338 --> 00:27:58.880
By the way, when we
scalarize, that's

00:27:58.880 --> 00:28:00.680
going to happen
automatically in a minute.

00:28:04.970 --> 00:28:05.470
OK.

00:28:05.470 --> 00:28:09.460
So now you have different names,
and these are famous names,

00:28:09.460 --> 00:28:15.070
so D optimal, duh, is going
to be the determinant.

00:28:15.070 --> 00:28:17.620
So if you minimize the log
determinant-- and that's

00:28:17.620 --> 00:28:18.490
actually beautiful.

00:28:18.490 --> 00:28:21.730
Basically, it says,
minimize the volume

00:28:21.730 --> 00:28:25.690
of a confidence
ellipsoid at some level

00:28:25.690 --> 00:28:26.950
because that's what it means.

00:28:26.950 --> 00:28:28.690
And by the way,
here your question--

00:28:28.690 --> 00:28:30.620
we can be very specific.

00:28:30.620 --> 00:28:33.400
If I choose, let's
say, lambda equals

00:28:33.400 --> 00:28:37.900
E1, that means I'm simply only
going to do experiment one.

00:28:37.900 --> 00:28:41.320
Then this matrix
is not invertible.

00:28:41.320 --> 00:28:43.540
I can take the minus
1 and put it out here,

00:28:43.540 --> 00:28:48.250
but then log determinant
of a non-singular matrix

00:28:48.250 --> 00:28:50.650
is going to be minus infinity.

00:28:50.650 --> 00:28:53.030
Of a singular matrix, it's
going to be minus infinity.

00:28:53.030 --> 00:28:56.410
And when I make it-- when I flip
the sign, I get plus infinity.

00:28:56.410 --> 00:28:59.350
And it's like, nice choice
of experiment design,

00:28:59.350 --> 00:29:00.970
but your objective is infinity.

00:29:00.970 --> 00:29:02.680
And given that
we're minimizing it,

00:29:02.680 --> 00:29:05.900
I would call that a
rather poor design.

00:29:05.900 --> 00:29:08.570
So I don't know if that
explained that, but OK.

00:29:08.570 --> 00:29:10.130
All right.

00:29:10.130 --> 00:29:11.893
So this is called
D-optimal experiment.

00:29:11.893 --> 00:29:13.310
You can have--
there's whole books

00:29:13.310 --> 00:29:15.710
on this, which is kind of
cool, I think, but that's OK.

00:29:19.250 --> 00:29:20.480
All right.

00:29:20.480 --> 00:29:23.190
You can actually work out
the dual problem of this,

00:29:23.190 --> 00:29:26.600
which is actually-- a dual--

00:29:26.600 --> 00:29:29.150
and it turns out to be
something that we're

00:29:29.150 --> 00:29:31.790
going to talk about
possibly even later today.

00:29:31.790 --> 00:29:32.940
But it looks like this.

00:29:32.940 --> 00:29:35.300
You're going to end up
with a maximize of the log

00:29:35.300 --> 00:29:36.470
determinant of W--

00:29:36.470 --> 00:29:38.360
W is going to be like
a dual variable--

00:29:38.360 --> 00:29:39.990
plus a constant.

00:29:39.990 --> 00:29:43.430
And then you're going to have
subject to this constant here.

00:29:43.430 --> 00:29:45.440
And this thing--
let's just check

00:29:45.440 --> 00:29:47.750
that is, in fact, a convex
optimization problem

00:29:47.750 --> 00:29:51.500
with variable capital W.

00:29:51.500 --> 00:29:53.240
This is concave.

00:29:53.240 --> 00:29:56.220
Log determinant of a positive
definite matrix is concave.

00:29:56.220 --> 00:29:59.140
That's a constant, doesn't
make any difference.

00:29:59.140 --> 00:30:04.080
What kind of inequality is
this as a function of W?

00:30:04.080 --> 00:30:05.860
What is it?

00:30:05.860 --> 00:30:06.490
It's linear.

00:30:06.490 --> 00:30:09.370
It's just linear
inequalities on W, quadratic

00:30:09.370 --> 00:30:12.070
in v's, but the v's
are given to us.

00:30:12.070 --> 00:30:16.930
Those are the options for
experiments that we carry out.

00:30:16.930 --> 00:30:18.820
And we can actually
interpret it really--

00:30:18.820 --> 00:30:19.490
this is cool.

00:30:19.490 --> 00:30:24.640
This is an ellipsoid, and
it's centered at the origin.

00:30:24.640 --> 00:30:26.390
And this simply says--

00:30:26.390 --> 00:30:33.400
this thing here says that all
the v's are in the ellipsoid,

00:30:33.400 --> 00:30:36.790
and so what this says
is, here's how you find--

00:30:36.790 --> 00:30:38.560
this tells you that
there's a relation

00:30:38.560 --> 00:30:42.460
between this experiment
design, and actually, you

00:30:42.460 --> 00:30:45.700
take your v's, then find the
minimum volume ellipsoid,

00:30:45.700 --> 00:30:47.980
send it to the origin
that covers them,

00:30:47.980 --> 00:30:49.970
and there's a close
relation to that.

00:30:49.970 --> 00:30:51.880
In fact, this weird
complementary slackness

00:30:51.880 --> 00:30:53.980
would tell you this, that
the one it touches-- when you

00:30:53.980 --> 00:30:55.450
find this minimum
volume ellipsoid,

00:30:55.450 --> 00:30:56.800
it touches some of the points.

00:30:56.800 --> 00:30:58.750
Those are the experiments
that are desirable.

00:30:58.750 --> 00:31:02.510
We'll look at some pictures
of that in a minute.

00:31:02.510 --> 00:31:03.790
Here's one.

00:31:03.790 --> 00:31:09.860
OK, so here's the
origin, and then here

00:31:09.860 --> 00:31:11.628
are the experiments
you've been offered.

00:31:11.628 --> 00:31:12.170
I don't know.

00:31:12.170 --> 00:31:15.620
Maybe there's 20 of them,
like 10 here and 10 here.

00:31:15.620 --> 00:31:16.970
And let's see.

00:31:16.970 --> 00:31:20.900
Let me ask a couple
of questions.

00:31:20.900 --> 00:31:25.280
OK, clearly these are
more desirable than these.

00:31:25.280 --> 00:31:26.270
How come?

00:31:26.270 --> 00:31:27.110
And just roughly.

00:31:30.995 --> 00:31:32.870
Why would these-- why
would these experiments

00:31:32.870 --> 00:31:34.625
be more powerful than these?

00:31:37.490 --> 00:31:38.622
More SNR?

00:31:38.622 --> 00:31:40.970
Yeah, they're farther
from the origin.

00:31:40.970 --> 00:31:42.230
That means vi is bigger.

00:31:42.230 --> 00:31:46.160
It means the signal-to-noise
ratio is higher.

00:31:46.160 --> 00:31:50.185
Now, here-- so this is the
minimum volume ellipsoid.

00:31:50.185 --> 00:31:51.560
You take an
ellipsoid, shrink it.

00:31:51.560 --> 00:31:53.480
We'll see how to do
that very shortly.

00:31:53.480 --> 00:31:55.825
You shrink it until
it just touches two,

00:31:55.825 --> 00:31:57.200
and that's this
one and this one.

00:31:57.200 --> 00:32:02.967
And it says that you should
use the high SNR options.

00:32:02.967 --> 00:32:03.800
That's what it says.

00:32:03.800 --> 00:32:05.915
And why does it pick
this one and that one?

00:32:09.010 --> 00:32:10.190
It's in two dimensions.

00:32:10.190 --> 00:32:10.690
How come?

00:32:10.690 --> 00:32:13.060
[INAUDIBLE]

00:32:13.060 --> 00:32:15.452
Yeah, that's right.

00:32:15.452 --> 00:32:17.410
You take the two
measurements-- the two highest

00:32:17.410 --> 00:32:21.940
signal-to-noise measurements
which are closest to orthogonal

00:32:21.940 --> 00:32:24.760
or they kind of give you
different information.

00:32:24.760 --> 00:32:28.000
If you're saying, this is the
way you should aim your radar,

00:32:28.000 --> 00:32:33.340
it says, yeah, ideally, I would
love to have this shot and then

00:32:33.340 --> 00:32:35.440
one 90 degrees, like that.

00:32:35.440 --> 00:32:37.840
That hasn't been
offered me here.

00:32:37.840 --> 00:32:39.100
Let me ask you a question.

00:32:39.100 --> 00:32:45.010
What if I shrunk these down
to just a little tiny arc?

00:32:45.010 --> 00:32:46.450
Someone guess.

00:32:46.450 --> 00:32:47.770
What do you think?

00:32:47.770 --> 00:32:50.650
Actually, I'd put
it somewhere else.

00:32:50.650 --> 00:32:52.540
It shouldn't reflect this thing.

00:32:52.540 --> 00:32:57.490
But suppose I put
these things way up,

00:32:57.490 --> 00:33:01.683
like over here, and I shrunk
them down to just a few?

00:33:01.683 --> 00:33:02.975
What do you think would happen?

00:33:06.095 --> 00:33:07.970
At some point, you
probably wouldn't take two

00:33:07.970 --> 00:33:11.380
because they don't have
enough different of a view,

00:33:11.380 --> 00:33:13.880
and that means that they're not
going to give you-- so you'd

00:33:13.880 --> 00:33:14.930
end up picking one of these.

00:33:14.930 --> 00:33:16.490
You don't want to,
but you'd pick it

00:33:16.490 --> 00:33:18.380
because you need this
kind of diversity

00:33:18.380 --> 00:33:20.660
to be able to see what it is.

00:33:20.660 --> 00:33:22.820
All this makes sense?

00:33:22.820 --> 00:33:24.350
OK.

00:33:24.350 --> 00:33:28.550
So this is the optimal
experiment design,

00:33:28.550 --> 00:33:30.577
and it says you should
make these both 0.5.

00:33:30.577 --> 00:33:31.160
Who knows why?

00:33:31.160 --> 00:33:33.440
Because maybe it's symmetric.

00:33:33.440 --> 00:33:36.290
By the way, there's a
ton of super-fun things

00:33:36.290 --> 00:33:37.820
you can add to this right.

00:33:37.820 --> 00:33:41.630
So here I just said, these
are your measurements.

00:33:41.630 --> 00:33:45.480
I could attach to each
measurement a cost,

00:33:45.480 --> 00:33:50.790
and I would say, I could
give you a budget in cost.

00:33:50.790 --> 00:33:53.190
The cost could be,
let's say, the energy

00:33:53.190 --> 00:33:56.430
you used to carry
out that experiment

00:33:56.430 --> 00:33:59.520
and you want to preserve
your drone battery life.

00:33:59.520 --> 00:34:02.330
Everybody following this?

00:34:02.330 --> 00:34:05.450
And that's-- actually, how
would I change the convex

00:34:05.450 --> 00:34:07.465
optimization problem to--

00:34:07.465 --> 00:34:08.840
how would I change
this for that?

00:34:11.679 --> 00:34:16.518
What would I do to
include a budget there?

00:34:16.518 --> 00:34:18.560
Then you can have all
sorts of interesting stuff.

00:34:18.560 --> 00:34:22.250
You could have really high
signal noise measurements,

00:34:22.250 --> 00:34:26.260
but they cost a lot of jewels.

00:34:26.260 --> 00:34:28.060
And these are not made up.

00:34:28.060 --> 00:34:30.190
This is exactly how things work.

00:34:30.190 --> 00:34:35.500
But any-- yeah?

00:34:35.500 --> 00:34:37.120
Would that be a
linear constraint?

00:34:37.120 --> 00:34:39.280
Yeah, this is a
linear-- its C transpose

00:34:39.280 --> 00:34:41.020
lambda is less than a budget.

00:34:41.020 --> 00:34:41.994
It's nothing more.

00:34:44.560 --> 00:34:47.320
You could do other weird stuff.

00:34:47.320 --> 00:34:49.210
Here's one just for fun.

00:34:49.210 --> 00:34:51.610
Let's suppose this is
actually a drone or something.

00:34:51.610 --> 00:34:54.310
Suppose it turns out that
you get a-- that it's free.

00:34:54.310 --> 00:34:58.920
Once you do one
experiment of one type,

00:34:58.920 --> 00:35:02.230
you get a discount
on, like, two others

00:35:02.230 --> 00:35:04.300
because once you
fly to that place,

00:35:04.300 --> 00:35:06.880
you might as well turn on your
LiDAR and blah, blah, blah

00:35:06.880 --> 00:35:09.640
and all these other things
or something like that.

00:35:09.640 --> 00:35:11.390
And we can just talked
briefly about that.

00:35:11.390 --> 00:35:12.515
How would you express that?

00:35:20.100 --> 00:35:21.360
How would you do that?

00:35:21.360 --> 00:35:22.290
I'll put it this way.

00:35:22.290 --> 00:35:24.120
I'll leave it for
you to think about.

00:35:24.120 --> 00:35:25.950
Those are convex
constraints, and they

00:35:25.950 --> 00:35:29.430
start getting really
sophisticated and quite

00:35:29.430 --> 00:35:30.030
interesting.

00:35:30.030 --> 00:35:33.260
Everybody understood
what I just said?

00:35:33.260 --> 00:35:36.660
So yeah, no, you get a-- it's
a weird thing where you say,

00:35:36.660 --> 00:35:38.317
well, if you use
that sensor, you

00:35:38.317 --> 00:35:39.650
might as well also use this one.

00:35:39.650 --> 00:35:40.442
You get a discount.

00:35:40.442 --> 00:35:42.020
It could even be free.

00:35:42.020 --> 00:35:47.450
So OK, I'll just leave
it this way for now.

00:35:47.450 --> 00:35:48.110
Yeah, OK.

00:35:48.110 --> 00:35:48.830
I'll leave it.

00:35:48.830 --> 00:35:51.530
We'll move on to the next topic.

00:35:51.530 --> 00:35:54.350
So let me just end
this by saying,

00:35:54.350 --> 00:35:57.920
the idea of this
experiment design stuff

00:35:57.920 --> 00:35:59.750
is this is extreme--

00:35:59.750 --> 00:36:04.100
this is only for
linear measurements

00:36:04.100 --> 00:36:06.290
with Gaussian noise.

00:36:06.290 --> 00:36:08.360
All other experiment
design, things

00:36:08.360 --> 00:36:10.280
get unbelievably complicated.

00:36:10.280 --> 00:36:12.410
They are basically
sequential decision-making

00:36:12.410 --> 00:36:14.120
under uncertainty
and things like that,

00:36:14.120 --> 00:36:16.170
and they start getting
really complicated.

00:36:16.170 --> 00:36:19.370
But for this one case, which is
actually kind of interesting--

00:36:19.370 --> 00:36:19.880
it is weird.

00:36:19.880 --> 00:36:21.380
If you think about
it, well, there's

00:36:21.380 --> 00:36:23.838
a lot of weird things about
Gaussians and things like that,

00:36:23.838 --> 00:36:27.560
but this basically says--

00:36:27.560 --> 00:36:30.260
this basically says, you
can say ahead of time

00:36:30.260 --> 00:36:31.970
how well you're going to do.

00:36:31.970 --> 00:36:35.073
If someone says,
there's my flight plan.

00:36:35.073 --> 00:36:37.740
What's going to be-- how well am
I going to estimate this thing?

00:36:37.740 --> 00:36:42.150
You can actually say ahead
of time, before doing it,

00:36:42.150 --> 00:36:43.642
whereas for other
things you can't

00:36:43.642 --> 00:36:44.850
do that for the distribution.

00:36:44.850 --> 00:36:48.150
Anyway, I just want to just
warn people about that.

00:36:48.150 --> 00:36:49.830
OK.

00:36:49.830 --> 00:36:52.350
I'm going to skip the
derivation of the dual,

00:36:52.350 --> 00:36:54.165
and we'll go on
to the next topic.

00:36:54.165 --> 00:36:54.805
Oh, question?

00:36:54.805 --> 00:36:55.305
Yeah?

00:36:55.305 --> 00:36:56.430
I have a quick question.

00:36:56.430 --> 00:36:56.930
Yeah?

00:36:56.930 --> 00:36:59.610
For the dual problem, how
do we get the minimum volume

00:36:59.610 --> 00:37:00.210
ellipsoid?

00:37:00.210 --> 00:37:01.440
Oh, you mean how--

00:37:01.440 --> 00:37:04.210
do you mean the
interpretation of it?

00:37:04.210 --> 00:37:06.090
It seems like a maximization--

00:37:06.090 --> 00:37:08.350
Yeah, well, so I
have to ask you,

00:37:08.350 --> 00:37:11.070
what is the volume
of this ellipsoid?

00:37:11.070 --> 00:37:12.510
That's going to
be our next topic.

00:37:12.510 --> 00:37:14.010
What's the volume
of this ellipsoid?

00:37:17.130 --> 00:37:18.960
OK, let's go real slow.

00:37:18.960 --> 00:37:23.940
If W is big like as a
matrix, what can you

00:37:23.940 --> 00:37:25.840
say about the ellipsoid there?

00:37:25.840 --> 00:37:27.580
It's small.

00:37:27.580 --> 00:37:28.765
So in fact, the volume--

00:37:28.765 --> 00:37:30.140
I don't even
remember what it is.

00:37:30.140 --> 00:37:35.080
It's like, it's the determinant
of W, I think, to the minus 1/2

00:37:35.080 --> 00:37:41.350
and then multiplied by the
volume of a unit ball in our--

00:37:41.350 --> 00:37:43.840
whatever it is,
in this case maybe

00:37:43.840 --> 00:37:47.710
our p or something
like that, which

00:37:47.710 --> 00:37:51.520
has got some gamma function
and some weird factorials

00:37:51.520 --> 00:37:55.090
and who knows, probably some
pies in there, I'm guessing.

00:37:55.090 --> 00:37:56.710
That doesn't matter.

00:37:56.710 --> 00:38:01.510
So the volume of this
is a constant times

00:38:01.510 --> 00:38:06.550
the determinant of W to the
minus 1/2, and that says--

00:38:06.550 --> 00:38:10.240
if you make-- so
minimizing that,

00:38:10.240 --> 00:38:13.540
minimizing determinant
of w to the minus 1

00:38:13.540 --> 00:38:17.860
over the square root of that, is
the same as maximizing the log

00:38:17.860 --> 00:38:19.440
determinant.

00:38:19.440 --> 00:38:22.080
So that's what that--
so I didn't say that,

00:38:22.080 --> 00:38:23.190
so it's kind of unclear.

00:38:23.190 --> 00:38:24.120
Yeah?

00:38:24.120 --> 00:38:26.640
I forgot that the big
W means the smallest.

00:38:26.640 --> 00:38:27.880
Right.

00:38:27.880 --> 00:38:30.940
That's for this-- that's
for this parametrization

00:38:30.940 --> 00:38:35.220
of an ellipsoid because we're
about to see some others.

00:38:35.220 --> 00:38:35.970
OK.

00:38:35.970 --> 00:38:37.380
Now we'll do geometric problems.

00:38:37.380 --> 00:38:39.542
Actually, I don't
even have-- here I'm

00:38:39.542 --> 00:38:41.250
going to mention some
really simple ones.

00:38:41.250 --> 00:38:44.050
They're so simple that it's
almost insulting to ask,

00:38:44.050 --> 00:38:46.780
but let's just start with
some really simple ones.

00:38:46.780 --> 00:38:48.780
Suppose I gave you
two ellipsoids--

00:38:48.780 --> 00:38:53.640
two polyhedra, like p is
the set of x for which Ax

00:38:53.640 --> 00:38:57.570
is less than b, and
let's say p tilde

00:38:57.570 --> 00:39:02.610
is the set of x for which A
tilde x is less than b tilde.

00:39:02.610 --> 00:39:05.670
So these are two--
these are two polyhedra.

00:39:05.670 --> 00:39:09.660
And suppose-- how would you
solve the following problems?

00:39:09.660 --> 00:39:11.610
How would you determine
if they intersect?

00:39:15.540 --> 00:39:16.680
Is it a convex problem?

00:39:16.680 --> 00:39:18.480
And what problem is it?

00:39:21.660 --> 00:39:24.370
It's a feasibility problem that
satisfies both constraints.

00:39:24.370 --> 00:39:24.870
That's it.

00:39:24.870 --> 00:39:26.440
It's just a feasibility problem.

00:39:26.440 --> 00:39:27.750
So you'd say, yes,
you can solve that.

00:39:27.750 --> 00:39:28.260
It's convex.

00:39:28.260 --> 00:39:29.880
It's an LP feasibility problem.

00:39:29.880 --> 00:39:31.800
OK, I'll take it up a notch.

00:39:31.800 --> 00:39:35.190
Find me the minimum distance
between the two polyhedra.

00:39:35.190 --> 00:39:36.630
Let me explain what that means.

00:39:40.470 --> 00:39:46.470
It means, find me a point in
one and a point in the other

00:39:46.470 --> 00:39:49.150
for which the distance
is as small as possible.

00:39:49.150 --> 00:39:50.430
What kind of problem is that?

00:39:54.060 --> 00:39:55.980
A squares with constraints.

00:39:55.980 --> 00:39:59.640
Yeah, it's just a--
it's a qp, right?

00:39:59.640 --> 00:40:01.560
I would say, it's going to be--

00:40:01.560 --> 00:40:03.690
I would have these
constraints, Ax less than b,

00:40:03.690 --> 00:40:07.080
and I would write Ax
tilde less than tilde.

00:40:07.080 --> 00:40:11.730
And I would minimize
simply x minus x tilde,

00:40:11.730 --> 00:40:15.810
there, any norm you like,
but we'll take 2-norm.

00:40:15.810 --> 00:40:16.750
Everybody got that?

00:40:16.750 --> 00:40:20.722
So these are just unbelievably
simple problems now.

00:40:20.722 --> 00:40:22.680
And what if the two--
what if the two polyhedra

00:40:22.680 --> 00:40:28.080
intersect and I ask you, find
the distance between the two

00:40:28.080 --> 00:40:30.770
polyhedra?

00:40:30.770 --> 00:40:31.650
It's a 0.

00:40:31.650 --> 00:40:34.062
Yeah, it's a 0, and
you name any point.

00:40:34.062 --> 00:40:35.520
And you go, wow,
how could it be 0?

00:40:35.520 --> 00:40:37.200
And you go, well,
because this one vector

00:40:37.200 --> 00:40:39.510
is in both polyhedra, and
the distance between a vector

00:40:39.510 --> 00:40:40.290
and itself is 0.

00:40:40.290 --> 00:40:41.190
Everybody got this?

00:40:41.190 --> 00:40:45.150
So these are simple
things, right?

00:40:45.150 --> 00:40:47.790
So we could go on and on.

00:40:47.790 --> 00:40:50.610
What if I represented
the polyhedra

00:40:50.610 --> 00:40:53.280
by a list of its vertices?

00:40:53.280 --> 00:41:03.780
Suppose I have p hat is the
convex hull of v1 up to vN?

00:41:03.780 --> 00:41:07.305
Now, how do I determine
if this intersects that?

00:41:09.830 --> 00:41:11.440
Any suggestions?

00:41:14.990 --> 00:41:17.090
It's a different
representation, right?

00:41:17.090 --> 00:41:21.250
So any suggestions?

00:41:21.250 --> 00:41:22.390
Is it a convex problem?

00:41:22.390 --> 00:41:25.260
How would you solve it?

00:41:25.260 --> 00:41:25.890
Yeah?

00:41:25.890 --> 00:41:27.557
You'd have [INAUDIBLE]
as your variable?

00:41:27.557 --> 00:41:28.740
Precisely.

00:41:28.740 --> 00:41:33.690
I would make one variable--
it's going to be some theta ivi.

00:41:33.690 --> 00:41:39.540
The theta i are positive,
and they sum to 1.

00:41:39.540 --> 00:41:42.570
That's my variable, theta.

00:41:42.570 --> 00:41:44.340
And if I want to--
if I want to do this,

00:41:44.340 --> 00:41:46.950
I would actually
write this equals x,

00:41:46.950 --> 00:41:50.990
and then I would also
have Ax less than b.

00:41:50.990 --> 00:41:52.780
Everybody see this?

00:41:52.780 --> 00:41:54.600
And so what that is is--

00:41:54.600 --> 00:41:58.180
and now I solve that
feasibility problem,

00:41:58.180 --> 00:42:01.380
which is an LP feasibility
problem, but really, who cares?

00:42:01.380 --> 00:42:02.580
I solved that problem.

00:42:02.580 --> 00:42:06.330
If this is feasible,
then this polyhedron

00:42:06.330 --> 00:42:09.360
described by its vertices
intersects this one described

00:42:09.360 --> 00:42:11.850
by its inequality constraints.

00:42:11.850 --> 00:42:15.210
If this is not feasible,
they don't intersect.

00:42:15.210 --> 00:42:18.210
Everything-- so anyway,
these are very basic ones.

00:42:18.210 --> 00:42:20.820
You can read about them
in the book, but in a way,

00:42:20.820 --> 00:42:22.680
at this point in the
course, you know enough

00:42:22.680 --> 00:42:25.180
that these are just almost
too simple to even--

00:42:25.180 --> 00:42:27.380
I mention them, but
that's the idea.

00:42:27.380 --> 00:42:32.980
So we are going to get to ones
where things are not obvious

00:42:32.980 --> 00:42:35.350
at all, and that's
actually the first topic.

00:42:35.350 --> 00:42:37.420
These are kind of
obvious, and you

00:42:37.420 --> 00:42:39.670
go on and on with different
geometrical problems.

00:42:39.670 --> 00:42:42.370
And these things obviously
come up all the time

00:42:42.370 --> 00:42:44.980
in things like robotics
if you want to know--

00:42:44.980 --> 00:42:47.770
if you have a polyhedral thing--

00:42:47.770 --> 00:42:51.040
if a polyhedron represents
a vehicle and then you have

00:42:51.040 --> 00:42:53.500
a polyhedral safe space or
something and you want to know,

00:42:53.500 --> 00:42:56.350
are you-- anyway, I
won't-- or an obstacle,

00:42:56.350 --> 00:42:59.170
and you want to know, am
I far, what's my distance

00:42:59.170 --> 00:43:00.580
to the obstacle, or something.

00:43:00.580 --> 00:43:01.150
OK.

00:43:01.150 --> 00:43:01.720
All right.

00:43:01.720 --> 00:43:05.080
So now we're going to look
at ones that are not obvious

00:43:05.080 --> 00:43:08.230
at all, and these
are going to be--

00:43:08.230 --> 00:43:10.600
I think these are
all pretty simple.

00:43:10.600 --> 00:43:14.410
This gets very weird
and cool, and it's

00:43:14.410 --> 00:43:15.620
more than 100 years old.

00:43:15.620 --> 00:43:17.080
So here it is.

00:43:17.080 --> 00:43:23.260
Suppose I have just a set
C in Rn, and what I want

00:43:23.260 --> 00:43:25.510
is I want to find
the minimum volume

00:43:25.510 --> 00:43:29.412
ellipsoid that covers it.

00:43:29.412 --> 00:43:34.360
So that's got a name.

00:43:34.360 --> 00:43:37.860
It's called the Lowner-John
ellipsoid of a set C.

00:43:37.860 --> 00:43:42.720
And now we're going to show you
how to pose that as a convex

00:43:42.720 --> 00:43:46.002
optimization problem,
and this is actually--

00:43:46.002 --> 00:43:46.710
it's interesting.

00:43:46.710 --> 00:43:49.080
Now you have to be very
careful because there's

00:43:49.080 --> 00:43:52.620
lots of parameterization
of ellipsoids,

00:43:52.620 --> 00:43:55.260
and for each of
these problems we're

00:43:55.260 --> 00:43:58.440
actually going to take
different parameterizations

00:43:58.440 --> 00:44:02.910
because in that parameterization
it's going to be convex,

00:44:02.910 --> 00:44:04.890
in another one it will not be.

00:44:04.890 --> 00:44:08.398
So you have to be completely
on your toes on these things.

00:44:08.398 --> 00:44:09.690
The truth is we already saw it.

00:44:09.690 --> 00:44:12.370
We didn't mention it
here, but for example,

00:44:12.370 --> 00:44:17.400
if I ask you to estimate the
covariance of a 0 mean Gaussian

00:44:17.400 --> 00:44:20.280
vector from data,
then it turns out

00:44:20.280 --> 00:44:25.890
the log likelihood is
not concave in sigma,

00:44:25.890 --> 00:44:27.300
the covariance matrix.

00:44:27.300 --> 00:44:31.537
It is concave in the inverse,
which is the precision matrix.

00:44:31.537 --> 00:44:33.370
So if you know what I'm
talking about, fine,

00:44:33.370 --> 00:44:34.950
and if you don't,
that's fine too.

00:44:34.950 --> 00:44:37.020
So that's a case
where there's lots

00:44:37.020 --> 00:44:39.540
of ways to say what a
covariance matrix is.

00:44:39.540 --> 00:44:42.630
You could give its
inverse, you could give it,

00:44:42.630 --> 00:44:45.930
and it's going to turn out
some things are convex in one

00:44:45.930 --> 00:44:48.060
but not the other and
so on and so forth.

00:44:48.060 --> 00:44:49.180
And this is similar.

00:44:49.180 --> 00:44:51.520
So here we go.

00:44:51.520 --> 00:44:52.980
So we're going to do this.

00:44:52.980 --> 00:44:55.470
I'm going to write
the ellipsoid.

00:44:55.470 --> 00:44:59.550
I'm going to parametrize it
by a matrix A and a vector b,

00:44:59.550 --> 00:45:05.010
and it's the inverse
image of the unit

00:45:05.010 --> 00:45:08.490
ball under an affine mapping.

00:45:08.490 --> 00:45:12.040
Everybody got that here?

00:45:12.040 --> 00:45:14.385
So that's what it's going to be.

00:45:17.280 --> 00:45:21.300
And here I'm going to say that
without loss of generality,

00:45:21.300 --> 00:45:23.940
I'm going to assume a is
symmetric and positive

00:45:23.940 --> 00:45:24.630
definite.

00:45:27.580 --> 00:45:30.370
So it has to be just positive
semidefefinite because I

00:45:30.370 --> 00:45:33.220
want it to be invertible here.

00:45:33.220 --> 00:45:34.870
Otherwise, that's
not an ellipsoid.

00:45:34.870 --> 00:45:37.480
It's got a-- whatever.

00:45:37.480 --> 00:45:40.260
It's got some infinite--
it's a cylinder I

00:45:40.260 --> 00:45:44.020
think is what people call
it or something like that.

00:45:44.020 --> 00:45:45.675
So I would like to know--

00:45:45.675 --> 00:45:47.800
if someone says, yeah, I
have a parameterization of

00:45:47.800 --> 00:45:51.190
ellipsoid with an and A and a
b, but my A is not symmetric,

00:45:51.190 --> 00:45:54.300
so I need someone to help
me to see why we would

00:45:54.300 --> 00:45:58.690
say without loss of generality.

00:45:58.690 --> 00:46:03.795
This is a subtlety
but an important one.

00:46:06.600 --> 00:46:12.540
So I have-- it's a set
of x for which Ax--

00:46:12.540 --> 00:46:13.830
is it plus or minus b?

00:46:13.830 --> 00:46:20.790
Ax plus b 2-norm is less than 1.

00:46:20.790 --> 00:46:24.300
Someone gives you this,
but A is not symmetric.

00:46:24.300 --> 00:46:26.490
Or it's not symmetric
and positive definite,

00:46:26.490 --> 00:46:28.080
so A is invertible.

00:46:28.080 --> 00:46:30.585
So any suggestions on
what we can do here?

00:46:35.970 --> 00:46:38.140
They give you a lower
triangular matrix.

00:46:38.140 --> 00:46:39.180
I don't know what they
give-- they give you

00:46:39.180 --> 00:46:40.013
something like that.

00:46:40.013 --> 00:46:42.860
So what do I do?

00:46:42.860 --> 00:46:44.780
Any-- yeah?

00:46:44.780 --> 00:46:47.300
Singular value decomposition?

00:46:47.300 --> 00:46:47.840
Yeah.

00:46:47.840 --> 00:46:52.730
Let's write U-- let's say
A is U sigma V transpose.

00:46:52.730 --> 00:46:57.140
Those are all square
because it's non-singular.

00:47:01.310 --> 00:47:04.760
Does everyone agree that
if I took this vector

00:47:04.760 --> 00:47:09.110
and I put in here an
orthogonal matrix then

00:47:09.110 --> 00:47:11.000
nothing has changed?

00:47:11.000 --> 00:47:15.740
I could put a Q in front
here, which is orthogonal,

00:47:15.740 --> 00:47:19.820
and this constraint is identical
because if I rotate something--

00:47:19.820 --> 00:47:22.700
and if I rotate the unit ball,
you still have the unit ball.

00:47:22.700 --> 00:47:24.470
Everybody cool with that?

00:47:24.470 --> 00:47:25.910
OK.

00:47:25.910 --> 00:47:29.075
Can someone suggest a Q
I should apply to this?

00:47:32.940 --> 00:47:35.670
And you want-- you
want to apply a Q which

00:47:35.670 --> 00:47:38.460
is going to have no
effect whatsoever on this

00:47:38.460 --> 00:47:41.130
but it's going to render
this thing-- when I multiply

00:47:41.130 --> 00:47:43.740
by Q on the left,
this thing is going

00:47:43.740 --> 00:47:46.800
to come out positive
definite and symmetric,

00:47:46.800 --> 00:47:52.440
so I'm open to
suggestions for what Q is.

00:47:52.440 --> 00:47:54.720
Q equals VU transpose?

00:47:54.720 --> 00:47:55.650
Yeah, there you go.

00:47:55.650 --> 00:47:58.650
Q equals VU transpose.

00:47:58.650 --> 00:47:59.760
That's orthogonal.

00:47:59.760 --> 00:48:01.080
These are both orthogonal.

00:48:01.080 --> 00:48:02.140
These are orthogonal.

00:48:02.140 --> 00:48:04.710
So if I plug that
in there, then A

00:48:04.710 --> 00:48:07.590
is now going to be V
sigma V transpose, which

00:48:07.590 --> 00:48:09.270
is symmetric and
positive definite.

00:48:09.270 --> 00:48:12.060
Everybody got this?

00:48:12.060 --> 00:48:14.130
OK, so this is--
it's a subtlety,

00:48:14.130 --> 00:48:17.250
but it's actually going
to be super important.

00:48:17.250 --> 00:48:19.620
And then it says the following.

00:48:19.620 --> 00:48:23.040
Oh, with this
parameterization, the volume

00:48:23.040 --> 00:48:26.460
is proportional to the
determinant of A inverse,

00:48:26.460 --> 00:48:27.900
and you can figure that out.

00:48:27.900 --> 00:48:32.490
If A gets big, then the
ellipsoid gets small.

00:48:32.490 --> 00:48:34.800
You can kind of see that, right?

00:48:34.800 --> 00:48:38.800
And so the volume is
proportional to det A inverse,

00:48:38.800 --> 00:48:41.250
and in fact, I think the
proportionality constant

00:48:41.250 --> 00:48:46.320
is this magic number, which
is the volume of a unit

00:48:46.320 --> 00:48:49.830
ball in Rn, which has some
gamma functions and probably

00:48:49.830 --> 00:48:51.300
some pies in it and other stuff.

00:48:51.300 --> 00:48:52.467
I don't-- it doesn't matter.

00:48:52.467 --> 00:48:55.710
It's a positive constant for us.

00:48:55.710 --> 00:49:01.410
So this thing is going to--

00:49:01.410 --> 00:49:06.690
this is the same as minimizing
the volume of E. Then

00:49:06.690 --> 00:49:09.360
the other thing we have
is we have to have that--

00:49:09.360 --> 00:49:13.320
every element of C has
to be in this ellipsoid,

00:49:13.320 --> 00:49:15.990
and the way to
express that is this.

00:49:15.990 --> 00:49:23.760
It says that if you maximize
over C Av plus b in 2-norm,

00:49:23.760 --> 00:49:25.290
if that's more
than 1, then you're

00:49:25.290 --> 00:49:28.440
not in-- you're not in-- you're
not in E. If it's less than

00:49:28.440 --> 00:49:31.350
or equal to 1, you're in
E. Everybody got that?

00:49:31.350 --> 00:49:33.090
And now we have
to stare at this,

00:49:33.090 --> 00:49:35.070
and you really have
to concentrate.

00:49:35.070 --> 00:49:37.050
Let me give you a
couple of the warnings

00:49:37.050 --> 00:49:38.910
about why you have
to concentrate now.

00:49:38.910 --> 00:49:40.950
Because the variables
in this problem

00:49:40.950 --> 00:49:44.640
are capital A and little b.

00:49:44.640 --> 00:49:48.600
The usual convention is that
a, b, c, d are like data,

00:49:48.600 --> 00:49:51.780
they're constant, and
the usual convention

00:49:51.780 --> 00:49:55.170
is the variables are
things like u, v, x, y, z.

00:49:55.170 --> 00:49:56.280
Everybody following this?

00:49:56.280 --> 00:49:58.800
That's usually.

00:49:58.800 --> 00:50:01.540
But here, well, v
is a dummy variable,

00:50:01.540 --> 00:50:06.160
so means absolutely
nothing, but here--

00:50:06.160 --> 00:50:08.160
so we have to ask,
is this thing--

00:50:08.160 --> 00:50:11.190
and A is, of course,
symmetric positive definite.

00:50:11.190 --> 00:50:15.630
This is convex log
determinant, A inverse.

00:50:15.630 --> 00:50:18.045
What kind of constraint
is this over A?

00:50:21.970 --> 00:50:23.140
It's convex.

00:50:23.140 --> 00:50:24.040
Why?

00:50:24.040 --> 00:50:29.140
Because if I fix a v,
what kind of function

00:50:29.140 --> 00:50:32.890
is that of A for a fixed v?

00:50:35.690 --> 00:50:37.018
It's convex in A and b.

00:50:37.018 --> 00:50:38.310
Sorry, it's jointly in A and b.

00:50:38.310 --> 00:50:40.110
It's convex.

00:50:40.110 --> 00:50:42.420
Matter of fact, the
argument here, Av plus b

00:50:42.420 --> 00:50:47.480
is a linear function of the
pair A, b, not even affine.

00:50:47.480 --> 00:50:48.950
This is linear.

00:50:48.950 --> 00:50:52.130
And so this thing,
for a fixed v,

00:50:52.130 --> 00:50:57.016
is a convex function
of the pair A, b.

00:50:57.016 --> 00:50:58.380
That's a supremum.

00:50:58.380 --> 00:51:01.130
Doesn't matter what C
is, that the result--

00:51:01.130 --> 00:51:03.680
this left-hand side is
a convex function here,

00:51:03.680 --> 00:51:07.370
so that's a convex function, and
it's less than or equal to 1.

00:51:07.370 --> 00:51:12.270
So whatever-- and this is a
convex optimization problem.

00:51:12.270 --> 00:51:14.670
OK, so this is how
you would minimize

00:51:14.670 --> 00:51:21.910
the volume of an ellipsoid
that covers a given set C.

00:51:21.910 --> 00:51:27.010
Now, we have to turn this into
something reasonable, something

00:51:27.010 --> 00:51:29.110
that we can handle,
and we'll do that.

00:51:29.110 --> 00:51:31.480
Here's a super-simple
example of finite set,

00:51:31.480 --> 00:51:35.050
and you have m points.

00:51:35.050 --> 00:51:38.470
And you end up with a
completely reasonable thing.

00:51:38.470 --> 00:51:40.060
It just looks like that.

00:51:40.060 --> 00:51:43.720
And that's a convex problem.

00:51:43.720 --> 00:51:47.050
You can solve it, and you
just get-- it just works.

00:51:47.050 --> 00:51:49.270
It just gives you the
minimum volume ellipsoid.

00:51:49.270 --> 00:51:52.810
So if I give you a bunch
of points-- by the way,

00:51:52.810 --> 00:51:55.415
that has a whole
lot of applications.

00:51:55.415 --> 00:51:56.665
I'll just mention one for fun.

00:51:59.290 --> 00:52:04.720
So here's a bunch of
points, like that,

00:52:04.720 --> 00:52:07.420
and then you-- oh God, I don't
know what I'm going to do here.

00:52:07.420 --> 00:52:10.337
So then you get some ellipsoid
that looks like that,

00:52:10.337 --> 00:52:12.670
and that's going to be the
minimum volume ellipsoid that

00:52:12.670 --> 00:52:14.380
covers those points.

00:52:14.380 --> 00:52:18.560
Everybody following this?

00:52:18.560 --> 00:52:21.950
So here's, actually,
an interesting way to--

00:52:21.950 --> 00:52:24.502
suppose I had-- suppose
these are like--

00:52:24.502 --> 00:52:26.210
in two dimensions it's
silly, but suppose

00:52:26.210 --> 00:52:31.310
it's in high dimensions and I
want to ask which of my data

00:52:31.310 --> 00:52:35.440
points here are
outliers, just like that.

00:52:35.440 --> 00:52:36.850
I don't have a
statistical model,

00:52:36.850 --> 00:52:39.100
although that would
be a reasonable way.

00:52:39.100 --> 00:52:40.660
So what would be
some reasonable ways

00:52:40.660 --> 00:52:42.460
to say something as an outlier?

00:52:46.735 --> 00:52:48.110
Close to the edge
of either side?

00:52:48.110 --> 00:52:48.650
It's what?

00:52:48.650 --> 00:52:49.490
It's close to the edge--

00:52:49.490 --> 00:52:51.490
Oh, but I haven't computed
this yet because we--

00:52:51.490 --> 00:52:53.690
let's just do-- like, how
would you do it otherwise?

00:52:53.690 --> 00:52:56.102
Well, you could
actually find the mean--

00:52:56.102 --> 00:52:56.810
here, here's one.

00:52:56.810 --> 00:53:01.010
You could find the mean and the
covariance of the data points,

00:53:01.010 --> 00:53:05.990
and then you'd look at the
so-called Mahalanobis distance

00:53:05.990 --> 00:53:07.548
or something from the center.

00:53:07.548 --> 00:53:08.840
That would be a reasonable way.

00:53:08.840 --> 00:53:11.570
Or you could look and just
say, well, which of your points

00:53:11.570 --> 00:53:13.070
are really big?

00:53:13.070 --> 00:53:15.860
Those are outliers.

00:53:15.860 --> 00:53:19.160
This is actually
a quite good one.

00:53:19.160 --> 00:53:22.400
It's entirely dependent
on the outliers

00:53:22.400 --> 00:53:24.170
because you can
kind of see that.

00:53:24.170 --> 00:53:27.650
If you shrink an ellipsoid
down, it's going to stop when,

00:53:27.650 --> 00:53:30.350
actually, generically,
nn plus 1 over 2,

00:53:30.350 --> 00:53:32.600
where, if that's the
dimension of points,

00:53:32.600 --> 00:53:35.600
are on that ellipsoid.

00:53:35.600 --> 00:53:38.210
So there's a method
for removing outliers

00:53:38.210 --> 00:53:41.000
called ellipsoidal peeling.

00:53:41.000 --> 00:53:42.050
It's awesome.

00:53:42.050 --> 00:53:44.295
First of all, it's a
great name, and it's also

00:53:44.295 --> 00:53:45.170
a pretty good method.

00:53:45.170 --> 00:53:46.280
And it works like this.

00:53:46.280 --> 00:53:47.870
You say, thanks for the data.

00:53:47.870 --> 00:53:49.490
You find the minimum
volume ellipsoid,

00:53:49.490 --> 00:53:53.480
and you find the
points that it touches.

00:53:53.480 --> 00:53:57.350
Those are the ones for
which this constraint here

00:53:57.350 --> 00:53:59.690
is equal to 1.

00:53:59.690 --> 00:54:04.160
Then you declare those to be
outliers, and you remove them,

00:54:04.160 --> 00:54:07.670
and you continue
until you like what

00:54:07.670 --> 00:54:09.982
happens, like, for example,
you're out of sample,

00:54:09.982 --> 00:54:11.690
whatever, if you have
some other test you

00:54:11.690 --> 00:54:14.390
can check-- your estimator gets
better or something like that.

00:54:14.390 --> 00:54:15.860
Everybody following this?

00:54:15.860 --> 00:54:17.180
OK.

00:54:17.180 --> 00:54:17.850
It's very cool.

00:54:17.850 --> 00:54:18.350
OK.

00:54:18.350 --> 00:54:21.650
Let me ask one
question just for fun.

00:54:21.650 --> 00:54:25.610
Suppose that you do that.

00:54:25.610 --> 00:54:28.820
And I have hundreds
and hundreds of points,

00:54:28.820 --> 00:54:33.928
but let's say that 25
satisfy this with equality.

00:54:33.928 --> 00:54:34.970
Everybody following this?

00:54:34.970 --> 00:54:35.810
25.

00:54:35.810 --> 00:54:37.730
And you'd say, I'm
going to remove them.

00:54:37.730 --> 00:54:39.050
There's 25 outliers.

00:54:39.050 --> 00:54:41.690
Then someone says, please don't.

00:54:41.690 --> 00:54:42.290
You go, why?

00:54:42.290 --> 00:54:45.080
Because, look, there's
no way we have 25--

00:54:45.080 --> 00:54:46.880
we actually only have five.

00:54:46.880 --> 00:54:49.250
Or I couldn't imagine
it being more than five.

00:54:49.250 --> 00:54:51.290
Everybody following this?

00:54:51.290 --> 00:54:54.050
Could someone please
suggest how you might choose

00:54:54.050 --> 00:54:57.640
the candidates to remove?

00:54:57.640 --> 00:54:58.930
Sounds like a trick question.

00:54:58.930 --> 00:55:01.860
It's actually not, and
it tells you if you can--

00:55:01.860 --> 00:55:05.940
it's easy if you can use the
material we've looked at.

00:55:05.940 --> 00:55:07.210
I compute the minimum volume--

00:55:07.210 --> 00:55:08.080
let's review.

00:55:08.080 --> 00:55:09.413
I have a whole bunch of vectors.

00:55:09.413 --> 00:55:13.020
I compute a minimum
volume ellipsoid.

00:55:13.020 --> 00:55:14.940
Let's say 22 of the--

00:55:14.940 --> 00:55:18.030
22 of the points are actually on
that minimum volume ellipsoid,

00:55:18.030 --> 00:55:22.380
meaning that constraint
is tight for 22.

00:55:22.380 --> 00:55:24.840
I say, take them
all out, and they

00:55:24.840 --> 00:55:27.165
go, no, you're throwing
away too much of my data.

00:55:31.610 --> 00:55:34.160
Eliminate the ones that are
on the far edge of the--

00:55:34.160 --> 00:55:36.260
Yeah, but what's the far
edge of an ellipsoid?

00:55:38.810 --> 00:55:41.420
No, I don't think that works.

00:55:41.420 --> 00:55:43.800
Is there another suggestion?

00:55:43.800 --> 00:55:46.040
You try to maximize
the difference

00:55:46.040 --> 00:55:47.810
in the volume from
the original to when

00:55:47.810 --> 00:55:50.390
you remove five of the points?

00:55:50.390 --> 00:55:53.725
Yeah, these are pretty--

00:55:53.725 --> 00:55:55.100
something like
that might happen.

00:55:55.100 --> 00:55:56.780
Was there another suggestion?

00:55:56.780 --> 00:55:57.790
Yeah?

00:55:57.790 --> 00:55:59.850
Just randomly throw out--

00:55:59.850 --> 00:56:01.670
OK, that would be fine.

00:56:01.670 --> 00:56:03.410
How about-- you say here--

00:56:03.410 --> 00:56:05.540
ellipsoid peeling
says that the first--

00:56:05.540 --> 00:56:06.770
these 20 are bad.

00:56:06.770 --> 00:56:09.470
And they go-- and they say,
no, no, no, I can only have--

00:56:09.470 --> 00:56:11.450
I can't imagine having
more than five outliers.

00:56:11.450 --> 00:56:14.540
And you go, cool, and you
just remove the first five.

00:56:14.540 --> 00:56:16.130
That's your suggestion?

00:56:16.130 --> 00:56:16.760
OK.

00:56:16.760 --> 00:56:17.490
I like it.

00:56:17.490 --> 00:56:20.195
That's a good street
fighting thing.

00:56:20.195 --> 00:56:21.912
You don't say it like
that to the person.

00:56:21.912 --> 00:56:23.870
You just go, hang on a
minute, and you go back,

00:56:23.870 --> 00:56:28.335
and you pretend to change
the code and stuff like that.

00:56:28.335 --> 00:56:29.210
And then you do that.

00:56:29.210 --> 00:56:31.750
That was another
suggestion somewhere.

00:56:31.750 --> 00:56:32.250
All right.

00:56:32.250 --> 00:56:39.030
Let me-- I try one more time to
see if I can elicit a response.

00:56:39.030 --> 00:56:43.220
I have 20 constraints
that are tight.

00:56:43.220 --> 00:56:46.520
I would like to remove five.

00:56:46.520 --> 00:56:48.620
In fact, I'll even--
do I even have to say?

00:56:48.620 --> 00:56:52.940
I'll even say, I want to remove
the five tightest constraints.

00:56:52.940 --> 00:56:55.200
How about that?

00:56:55.200 --> 00:56:56.155
Please?

00:56:56.155 --> 00:56:56.655
What?

00:56:56.655 --> 00:56:58.140
Would that be the
dual variables?

00:56:58.140 --> 00:56:58.980
Oh, there we go.

00:56:58.980 --> 00:56:59.580
OK, fine.

00:56:59.580 --> 00:57:02.040
You get the dual variables,
which you get for free,

00:57:02.040 --> 00:57:06.930
and then you remove the
five with the five largest

00:57:06.930 --> 00:57:09.090
dual variables.

00:57:09.090 --> 00:57:12.577
Everybody cool?

00:57:12.577 --> 00:57:14.910
That's a pretty sophisticated--
we're just putting ideas

00:57:14.910 --> 00:57:16.120
from the class together.

00:57:16.120 --> 00:57:17.940
This is not-- but
things like this

00:57:17.940 --> 00:57:19.530
are actually kind of useful.

00:57:19.530 --> 00:57:21.930
Actually, that was
a good question

00:57:21.930 --> 00:57:26.760
because it's the ones we
like because I didn't say

00:57:26.760 --> 00:57:28.890
the D word, which is duality.

00:57:28.890 --> 00:57:31.110
But that was a
question about duality,

00:57:31.110 --> 00:57:35.130
so what we want is we want
you to have a street fighting

00:57:35.130 --> 00:57:37.367
knowledge of duality
because it never ever works

00:57:37.367 --> 00:57:39.450
when someone comes and
says, oh, help me, help me,

00:57:39.450 --> 00:57:40.320
I have a dual.

00:57:40.320 --> 00:57:42.000
And you're like,
no, it doesn't work

00:57:42.000 --> 00:57:43.890
that-- the way it
always works is somebody

00:57:43.890 --> 00:57:45.630
has some long,
complicated problem,

00:57:45.630 --> 00:57:47.230
and they need a suggestion.

00:57:47.230 --> 00:57:50.310
And they don't even
know what duality is.

00:57:50.310 --> 00:57:50.970
Everyone cool?

00:57:50.970 --> 00:57:53.385
That was just an aside, but
it was fun, for me anyway.

00:57:55.920 --> 00:57:56.760
OK.

00:57:56.760 --> 00:58:00.780
So this gives you the
minimum volume ellipsoid.

00:58:00.780 --> 00:58:01.320
OK.

00:58:01.320 --> 00:58:05.370
So then you can say,
OK, fine, can we do--

00:58:05.370 --> 00:58:06.630
or let me just ask--

00:58:06.630 --> 00:58:09.263
I'm going to go back and
show one of the-- so you say,

00:58:09.263 --> 00:58:10.930
that's cool, you can
do a minimum volume

00:58:10.930 --> 00:58:12.220
ellipsoid around a point.

00:58:12.220 --> 00:58:16.120
And then you might innocently
ask, well, that's cool.

00:58:16.120 --> 00:58:19.690
By the way, this is the
minimum volume ellipsoid

00:58:19.690 --> 00:58:26.695
that covers a polyhedron
given by its vertices.

00:58:26.695 --> 00:58:27.820
So that's the same problem.

00:58:27.820 --> 00:58:29.110
You agree?

00:58:29.110 --> 00:58:30.520
So if someone walks up to
you in the street and says,

00:58:30.520 --> 00:58:32.395
find the minimum volume
ellipsoid that covers

00:58:32.395 --> 00:58:38.410
a polyhedron described by its
vertices, then that's-- also,

00:58:38.410 --> 00:58:39.280
we can do that.

00:58:39.280 --> 00:58:42.280
It's a simple convex problem.

00:58:42.280 --> 00:58:42.850
You cool?

00:58:42.850 --> 00:58:45.490
But now someone says, oh,
yeah, you know what, though?

00:58:45.490 --> 00:58:49.570
My ellipsoid is described
by not vertices,

00:58:49.570 --> 00:58:51.680
but it looks like this.

00:58:51.680 --> 00:58:52.780
Here's my ellipsoid.

00:58:52.780 --> 00:58:59.110
It's described by a set
of linear inequalities,

00:58:59.110 --> 00:59:01.720
and we want to find
the minimum volume

00:59:01.720 --> 00:59:08.460
ellipsoid that covers
a polyhedron described

00:59:08.460 --> 00:59:09.810
by its faces.

00:59:09.810 --> 00:59:12.150
Everybody cool on this?

00:59:12.150 --> 00:59:13.490
OK.

00:59:13.490 --> 00:59:14.660
It sounds reasonable.

00:59:14.660 --> 00:59:17.750
Actually, someone
give a simple--

00:59:17.750 --> 00:59:19.850
how would you-- what
would be one way to do it?

00:59:22.380 --> 00:59:23.780
What would you do?

00:59:23.780 --> 00:59:26.850
In the intersection of all
the inequalities in the m.

00:59:26.850 --> 00:59:30.690
Yeah, so you would basically--
you'd first write a method that

00:59:30.690 --> 00:59:35.100
called faces to vertices.

00:59:35.100 --> 00:59:38.070
And you'd take your ellipsoid,
and you'd call the faces

00:59:38.070 --> 00:59:39.942
to vertices method on it.

00:59:39.942 --> 00:59:41.400
And it would give
you the vertices,

00:59:41.400 --> 00:59:43.450
and then you'd call--
then you'd type this in.

00:59:43.450 --> 00:59:44.913
It's three lines of Cvx pi.

00:59:44.913 --> 00:59:46.080
That's your proposal, right?

00:59:46.080 --> 00:59:46.590
Right.

00:59:46.590 --> 00:59:47.190
I like it.

00:59:47.190 --> 00:59:48.510
It would work.

00:59:48.510 --> 00:59:52.840
There's one minor problem with
that is how many vertices,

00:59:52.840 --> 00:59:58.380
just order of magnitude, does
a polyhedron in R 1,000 with,

00:59:58.380 --> 01:00:02.620
let's say, 5,000 inequalities,
which, by the way,

01:00:02.620 --> 01:00:05.550
is a tiny thing you can solve
on-- you can solve in LP

01:00:05.550 --> 01:00:07.338
or problems with
that on your phone--

01:00:07.338 --> 01:00:08.130
everybody got that?

01:00:08.130 --> 01:00:11.310
How many vertices does that have
about, just order of magnitude?

01:00:14.170 --> 01:00:15.928
Everybody know what I'm saying?

01:00:15.928 --> 01:00:17.470
I'm not drawing a
picture here in R2.

01:00:17.470 --> 01:00:19.840
I'm talking about R
1,000 or whatever,

01:00:19.840 --> 01:00:21.610
and you've got
5,000 inequalities.

01:00:21.610 --> 01:00:23.860
How many vertices does it have?

01:00:23.860 --> 01:00:24.730
A lot.

01:00:24.730 --> 01:00:25.270
A lot.

01:00:25.270 --> 01:00:26.680
Yeah, that's the answer.

01:00:26.680 --> 01:00:28.810
It's order of magnitude
1,000 choose--

01:00:28.810 --> 01:00:31.060
whatever, 5,000 choose 1,000.

01:00:31.060 --> 01:00:34.480
Just for the record,
that's a super-big number.

01:00:34.480 --> 01:00:36.040
So your method was correct.

01:00:36.040 --> 01:00:39.340
It would work in two dimensions,
in three, in blah, blah, blah.

01:00:39.340 --> 01:00:42.590
It's not going to-- it
would be absolutely correct.

01:00:42.590 --> 01:00:45.260
It just wouldn't work.

01:00:45.260 --> 01:00:45.760
OK.

01:00:45.760 --> 01:00:46.360
Everybody got that?

01:00:46.360 --> 01:00:46.860
OK.

01:00:46.860 --> 01:00:48.790
So now I'll let you
in on the secret.

01:00:48.790 --> 01:00:52.720
It turns out, actually, finding
the minimum volume ellipsoid

01:00:52.720 --> 01:00:58.170
that covers a polyhedron given
an inequality form is NP-hard.

01:00:58.170 --> 01:01:00.222
As a matter of fact,
the following is.

01:01:00.222 --> 01:01:01.680
I just walk up to
you in the street

01:01:01.680 --> 01:01:03.360
and say, here's an ellipsoid.

01:01:03.360 --> 01:01:04.980
Like, don't even optimize it.

01:01:04.980 --> 01:01:06.500
Here's an ellipsoid,
and I want to--

01:01:06.500 --> 01:01:08.250
I ask you the following
innocent question.

01:01:08.250 --> 01:01:13.870
Does my ellipsoid
cover this polyhedron?

01:01:13.870 --> 01:01:15.250
Makes sense, right?

01:01:15.250 --> 01:01:17.920
So you could even imagine
all sorts of-- you

01:01:17.920 --> 01:01:19.510
could imagine all
sorts of things

01:01:19.510 --> 01:01:21.040
that people might use this for.

01:01:21.040 --> 01:01:25.060
They would say, this
is a-- my polyhedron

01:01:25.060 --> 01:01:26.977
is my desired region
or something like that,

01:01:26.977 --> 01:01:28.810
and I'm going to cover
it with an ellipsoid.

01:01:28.810 --> 01:01:29.980
You just want to ask,
does it cover it?

01:01:29.980 --> 01:01:31.510
Anyway, everyone
got the question?

01:01:31.510 --> 01:01:36.000
Unbelievably simple
to state, NP-hard.

01:01:36.000 --> 01:01:41.230
So it gets very-- these things
are about to get very weird.

01:01:41.230 --> 01:01:44.290
So it depends on how you
describe the polyhedron and all

01:01:44.290 --> 01:01:49.120
that kind of stuff, so,
yeah, if you're confused,

01:01:49.120 --> 01:01:54.910
you should be because
this is quite complicated.

01:01:54.910 --> 01:01:58.480
Now to confuse you-- if
you're not yet confused,

01:01:58.480 --> 01:02:00.260
this should do the trick.

01:02:00.260 --> 01:02:03.580
So now we go to something
like a dual problem.

01:02:03.580 --> 01:02:06.640
It turns out they're not
really duals, but that's OK.

01:02:06.640 --> 01:02:09.830
The dual problem is,
I have my set C here.

01:02:09.830 --> 01:02:13.720
I'll just draw a C. Here's C.
And what I want to do now-- we

01:02:13.720 --> 01:02:16.967
just-- the Lowner-John
ellipsoid is,

01:02:16.967 --> 01:02:18.550
find the minimum
volume ellipsoid that

01:02:18.550 --> 01:02:19.855
covers this thing.

01:02:22.450 --> 01:02:24.850
Now this is a variation on that.

01:02:24.850 --> 01:02:27.490
This doesn't have a person's
name attached to it,

01:02:27.490 --> 01:02:29.680
or I guess I should
say, that's this one.

01:02:29.680 --> 01:02:32.290
It says, find me the
maximum volume ellipsoid

01:02:32.290 --> 01:02:33.880
that fits inside your set.

01:02:37.030 --> 01:02:38.440
Here's what's going to happen.

01:02:38.440 --> 01:02:39.180
It's crazy.

01:02:39.180 --> 01:02:42.900
If the set is an ellipsoid--
if the set is a polyhedron,

01:02:42.900 --> 01:02:45.450
we're going to be able
to solve this one when

01:02:45.450 --> 01:02:49.560
these are given by inequalities,
totally straightforward.

01:02:49.560 --> 01:02:51.240
But if it's given
by the vertices,

01:02:51.240 --> 01:02:53.200
it's going to be NP-hard.

01:02:53.200 --> 01:02:55.500
So it's just weird and crazy.

01:02:55.500 --> 01:02:59.340
I can calculate the
maximum volume ellipsoid

01:02:59.340 --> 01:03:04.450
that fits inside a polyhedron
described by inequalities.

01:03:04.450 --> 01:03:06.960
That's easy, tractable.

01:03:06.960 --> 01:03:09.630
I can find the minimum
volume ellipsoid

01:03:09.630 --> 01:03:12.780
that covers a polyhedron
described by its vertices,

01:03:12.780 --> 01:03:14.010
totally tractable.

01:03:14.010 --> 01:03:15.060
Everybody following?

01:03:15.060 --> 01:03:20.330
But the two other problems are
actually extremely difficult.

01:03:20.330 --> 01:03:22.030
Everybody got that?

01:03:22.030 --> 01:03:25.810
So it's interesting, actually.

01:03:25.810 --> 01:03:27.065
So let's see how to do that.

01:03:27.065 --> 01:03:28.690
Now, to do that, what
we're going to do

01:03:28.690 --> 01:03:30.357
is we're going to use
a-- we're actually

01:03:30.357 --> 01:03:32.900
going to use the opposite
parameterization we did before.

01:03:32.900 --> 01:03:35.410
Before, to find the
minimum volume ellipsoid,

01:03:35.410 --> 01:03:37.570
we use the inverse
image of the unit ball

01:03:37.570 --> 01:03:39.400
under an affine mapping.

01:03:39.400 --> 01:03:44.230
Here it's going to be the
forward image of a unit ball,

01:03:44.230 --> 01:03:46.120
so here's the unit
ball, and I'm going

01:03:46.120 --> 01:03:49.510
to make a forward image
under an affine mapping.

01:03:49.510 --> 01:03:51.670
So here, by the way,
the bigger B is,

01:03:51.670 --> 01:03:55.780
the bigger E is,
whereas the opposite

01:03:55.780 --> 01:03:57.910
was true for the previous
parameterization.

01:03:57.910 --> 01:04:02.140
So we do this, and
here, once again, we

01:04:02.140 --> 01:04:04.120
can assume without
any loss of generality

01:04:04.120 --> 01:04:07.750
that B is both symmetric
and positive definite.

01:04:07.750 --> 01:04:09.970
The B is invertible,
but I can do that--

01:04:09.970 --> 01:04:11.350
and I do the same trick.

01:04:11.350 --> 01:04:14.410
Here, if I put a
orthogonal matrix

01:04:14.410 --> 01:04:17.110
after-- if I replace
b with Bq, the problem

01:04:17.110 --> 01:04:20.435
is identical because
q multiplies u.

01:04:20.435 --> 01:04:24.253
u is in the unit ball, and if
I have an orthogonal matrix,

01:04:24.253 --> 01:04:25.670
it doesn't change
anything at all.

01:04:25.670 --> 01:04:29.390
Everybody following this?

01:04:29.390 --> 01:04:32.540
So that's my parameterization,
and now the volume

01:04:32.540 --> 01:04:35.010
is proportional to
the determinant of B.

01:04:35.010 --> 01:04:37.910
So if we maximize log
determinant B, that's

01:04:37.910 --> 01:04:41.625
the same as maximizing
determinant B,

01:04:41.625 --> 01:04:45.600
and that's actually-- log
determinant is, as you know,

01:04:45.600 --> 01:04:46.530
concave.

01:04:46.530 --> 01:04:49.800
And then we're maximizing,
so everything is cool.

01:04:49.800 --> 01:04:53.190
Everybody following this?

01:04:53.190 --> 01:04:57.540
OK, now we have to
be able to say that--

01:04:57.540 --> 01:05:01.020
we have to be able to say
that the following is true.

01:05:01.020 --> 01:05:05.850
I want to say that everything
in this ellipsoid is inside--

01:05:05.850 --> 01:05:10.530
is inside my set
because my ellipsoid

01:05:10.530 --> 01:05:11.700
has to fit inside the set.

01:05:11.700 --> 01:05:14.880
And the way you
test that is this,

01:05:14.880 --> 01:05:20.360
is I'll take the
indicator function for C,

01:05:20.360 --> 01:05:24.950
and that's just something that's
0 if you're in C and infinity

01:05:24.950 --> 01:05:25.520
otherwise.

01:05:25.520 --> 01:05:26.540
And I write it this way.

01:05:26.540 --> 01:05:30.960
So IC of Bu plus d means--

01:05:30.960 --> 01:05:31.460
let's see.

01:05:31.460 --> 01:05:32.580
What does it mean?

01:05:32.580 --> 01:05:36.050
It means if this element is
in C, this is plus infinity.

01:05:36.050 --> 01:05:37.040
Otherwise, this is 0.

01:05:40.150 --> 01:05:43.030
And this is silly to
say sup u less than--

01:05:43.030 --> 01:05:45.520
it just says that
basically for all--

01:05:45.520 --> 01:05:47.740
it says, basically,
everything of the form

01:05:47.740 --> 01:05:51.370
Bu plus d with norm 2
less than 1 is in the set.

01:05:51.370 --> 01:05:53.650
That's just a fancy
way to write it.

01:05:53.650 --> 01:05:55.380
OK.

01:05:55.380 --> 01:05:59.470
Now, this is actually-- this is
a convex optimization problem,

01:05:59.470 --> 01:06:02.640
but once again, we have
kind of a weird supremum

01:06:02.640 --> 01:06:06.180
or infimum in the problem.

01:06:06.180 --> 01:06:09.690
We know it's a convex, but we
have no way to write it down.

01:06:09.690 --> 01:06:10.553
It's not tractable.

01:06:10.553 --> 01:06:11.970
And so actually,
this is the point

01:06:11.970 --> 01:06:13.680
in the course when
we can start--

01:06:13.680 --> 01:06:19.170
basically, level 0 is
convex optimization problems

01:06:19.170 --> 01:06:20.340
are tractable.

01:06:20.340 --> 01:06:22.860
That's like, if someone
on the street asks you

01:06:22.860 --> 01:06:25.140
or whatever or from
a different field

01:06:25.140 --> 01:06:26.940
asks you, who cares
about it, and you

01:06:26.940 --> 01:06:28.890
go, well, they're tractable.

01:06:28.890 --> 01:06:30.900
But now you can see
that's actually false

01:06:30.900 --> 01:06:33.070
because this is an example--

01:06:33.070 --> 01:06:34.792
this is an example
of a problem where--

01:06:34.792 --> 01:06:36.000
you just have some tractable.

01:06:36.000 --> 01:06:37.958
Actually, the ones I said
were NP-hard before--

01:06:37.958 --> 01:06:40.050
they're convex
problems, no problem.

01:06:40.050 --> 01:06:41.280
They just have this--

01:06:41.280 --> 01:06:43.740
they just have this infimum
or supremum in there

01:06:43.740 --> 01:06:47.130
that you can't represent
right, so yeah.

01:06:47.130 --> 01:06:47.950
So it depends.

01:06:47.950 --> 01:06:49.410
You have to judge the social--

01:06:49.410 --> 01:06:51.540
it depends on the
social setting.

01:06:51.540 --> 01:06:53.730
If you're with a bunch of
mathematicians or people

01:06:53.730 --> 01:06:56.680
who know a lot, then
you don't have to--

01:06:56.680 --> 01:07:00.760
you wouldn't say all convex
problems are tractable.

01:07:00.760 --> 01:07:01.480
OK.

01:07:01.480 --> 01:07:03.310
All right.

01:07:03.310 --> 01:07:07.690
So here let's do a polyhedron,
and then I can actually--

01:07:07.690 --> 01:07:09.010
then I can check.

01:07:09.010 --> 01:07:12.040
So with this parameterization,
if I want to know

01:07:12.040 --> 01:07:17.020
is E inside this polyhedron
given by a set of inequalities,

01:07:17.020 --> 01:07:19.120
now we can-- that's actually
completely tractable,

01:07:19.120 --> 01:07:20.440
and it looks like this.

01:07:20.440 --> 01:07:22.720
That's a perfectly good
problem right here.

01:07:22.720 --> 01:07:25.660
Maximize the log
determinant subject to--

01:07:25.660 --> 01:07:26.890
and let's be very careful.

01:07:26.890 --> 01:07:29.200
You have to be really
on your toes here.

01:07:29.200 --> 01:07:31.060
What are the variables
in this problem?

01:07:36.690 --> 01:07:39.208
What are the variables?

01:07:39.208 --> 01:07:39.708
Capital B.

01:07:39.708 --> 01:07:42.167
Capital B, a little d.

01:07:42.167 --> 01:07:42.750
Is that right?

01:07:42.750 --> 01:07:43.050
Yeah.

01:07:43.050 --> 01:07:43.550
OK.

01:07:43.550 --> 01:07:47.615
And then what are ai and bi?

01:07:47.615 --> 01:07:48.530
What are they?

01:07:48.530 --> 01:07:49.390
Constants.

01:07:49.390 --> 01:07:53.780
Data or constants or parameters,
however you want to say it.

01:07:53.780 --> 01:07:54.280
OK.

01:07:54.280 --> 01:07:58.210
So then what kind of
function is of the variables?

01:08:01.200 --> 01:08:02.500
It's convex.

01:08:02.500 --> 01:08:08.107
Bai is linear, and then it's
a 2-norm, so that's convex.

01:08:08.107 --> 01:08:10.065
What kind of function of
the variables is that?

01:08:13.310 --> 01:08:17.540
It's affine, but it's
linear, so, again, it

01:08:17.540 --> 01:08:20.810
is affine, of course,
but it's even linear.

01:08:20.810 --> 01:08:22.609
And so this is a
convex constraint

01:08:22.609 --> 01:08:24.830
because that's a
convex function.

01:08:24.830 --> 01:08:29.029
In fact, it's second-order
cone representable.

01:08:29.029 --> 01:08:31.069
OK.

01:08:31.069 --> 01:08:32.270
So that's it.

01:08:32.270 --> 01:08:36.630
So this crazy thing where
if I give a polyhedron,

01:08:36.630 --> 01:08:41.450
if I give you a polyhedron
described by inequalities,

01:08:41.450 --> 01:08:44.580
we can find the maximum
volume ellipsoid inside it.

01:08:44.580 --> 01:08:45.620
Everybody got that?

01:08:45.620 --> 01:08:47.149
And that's completely tractable.

01:08:47.149 --> 01:08:50.657
By the way, this has tons
of applications immediately.

01:08:50.657 --> 01:08:52.490
Like, suppose I give
you a polyhedron that's

01:08:52.490 --> 01:08:55.220
very complicated defined by
inequalities and you say,

01:08:55.220 --> 01:08:57.439
that's a safe operating region.

01:08:57.439 --> 01:08:59.923
If your state or whatever it
is, if your robot or whatever,

01:08:59.923 --> 01:09:02.090
if you're in that, you're
not going to hit anything,

01:09:02.090 --> 01:09:02.810
and it's safe.

01:09:02.810 --> 01:09:05.416
Everybody following this?

01:09:05.416 --> 01:09:08.960
This allows you to do something
like make a guaranteed--

01:09:08.960 --> 01:09:11.694
the maximum volume
safe ellipsoid.

01:09:11.694 --> 01:09:13.319
And someone say, why
would you do that?

01:09:13.319 --> 01:09:15.240
You say, because
my algorithm that

01:09:15.240 --> 01:09:20.670
manipulates it can handle an
ellipsoid but not a polyhedron

01:09:20.670 --> 01:09:22.620
with 80 faces or
something like--

01:09:22.620 --> 01:09:24.270
everybody following this?

01:09:24.270 --> 01:09:26.319
So this already
has a lot of uses.

01:09:26.319 --> 01:09:30.000
That's one just right there.

01:09:30.000 --> 01:09:32.479
OK.

01:09:32.479 --> 01:09:35.840
Now, it turns out that something
very weird about these.

01:09:35.840 --> 01:09:36.559
It's very cool.

01:09:39.439 --> 01:09:42.490
This is math, but it's
actually super interesting

01:09:42.490 --> 01:09:43.979
and has all sorts
of implications.

01:09:43.979 --> 01:09:45.140
So here it is.

01:09:45.140 --> 01:09:49.100
Take any convex, bounded
set with non-empty interior.

01:09:49.100 --> 01:09:50.395
So it's not flat.

01:09:50.395 --> 01:09:52.520
It doesn't go off to infinity
and blah, blah, blah.

01:09:52.520 --> 01:09:54.109
There's C.

01:09:54.109 --> 01:09:56.900
Then the Lowner-John--
by the way,

01:09:56.900 --> 01:09:59.150
I'm not-- this is not--
we're not talking about

01:09:59.150 --> 01:10:01.295
whether or not you can
compute the minimum volume

01:10:01.295 --> 01:10:03.920
ellipsoid that covers something
or the maximum volume ellipsoid

01:10:03.920 --> 01:10:04.520
inside it.

01:10:04.520 --> 01:10:05.750
That's a separate question.

01:10:05.750 --> 01:10:08.450
This is math, so we
don't we're unbothered

01:10:08.450 --> 01:10:10.010
by the questions of--

01:10:10.010 --> 01:10:12.290
we're just looking at what's
true and false, not how

01:10:12.290 --> 01:10:12.890
do you--

01:10:12.890 --> 01:10:15.680
how much trouble is
it to compute it.

01:10:15.680 --> 01:10:16.220
OK.

01:10:16.220 --> 01:10:19.700
So it turns out that if you
take the Lowner-John ellipsoid--

01:10:19.700 --> 01:10:23.090
so that says, take your set.

01:10:23.090 --> 01:10:26.450
You find the minimum volume
ellipsoid that covers your set.

01:10:26.450 --> 01:10:29.510
Then it says, shrink
the set around--

01:10:29.510 --> 01:10:33.750
shrink that ellipsoid around
its center by a factor n.

01:10:33.750 --> 01:10:35.130
Everybody got that?

01:10:35.130 --> 01:10:38.790
That's inside the set.

01:10:38.790 --> 01:10:41.550
That's what it--
that's what it says.

01:10:41.550 --> 01:10:45.990
And the opposite is true for
the maximum volume ellipsoid.

01:10:45.990 --> 01:10:48.060
If I have any set
at all, convex,

01:10:48.060 --> 01:10:51.800
I find the maximum volume
ellipsoid that fits inside it.

01:10:51.800 --> 01:10:56.270
Now I expand it by a
factor n about the center.

01:10:56.270 --> 01:10:59.100
That covers the set.

01:10:59.100 --> 01:11:00.330
And it gets even weirder.

01:11:00.330 --> 01:11:04.860
If the set is symmetric,
like if minus the set is

01:11:04.860 --> 01:11:07.968
equal to itself or something
like that, then it turns out

01:11:07.968 --> 01:11:09.510
you don't have to
have a factor of n.

01:11:09.510 --> 01:11:11.070
You have a factor
of square root n.

01:11:11.070 --> 01:11:13.080
Everybody following this?

01:11:13.080 --> 01:11:15.300
Now, this actually tells
you unbelievable things.

01:11:15.300 --> 01:11:17.880
This says that if
you had a convex set

01:11:17.880 --> 01:11:23.265
and you approximate it with an
ellipsoid, it says you really--

01:11:23.265 --> 01:11:24.970
you can't be too far off.

01:11:24.970 --> 01:11:27.630
In fact, if I take, let's
say, either the Lowner-John--

01:11:27.630 --> 01:11:30.210
let's suppose I take the
Lowner-John ellipsoid and I

01:11:30.210 --> 01:11:31.410
shrink it by--

01:11:31.410 --> 01:11:36.170
or suppose I shrink
it by square root n

01:11:36.170 --> 01:11:38.720
and I say, that's the
ellipsoidal approximation

01:11:38.720 --> 01:11:41.450
of my set, and you go, cool.

01:11:41.450 --> 01:11:43.800
In which-- how does it
approximate your set?

01:11:43.800 --> 01:11:46.460
You go, well, if I expand
it by factor square root n,

01:11:46.460 --> 01:11:47.630
it covers my set.

01:11:47.630 --> 01:11:49.640
If I shrink it by a
factor of square root n,

01:11:49.640 --> 01:11:51.350
it's inside the set.

01:11:51.350 --> 01:11:52.772
Everybody got it?

01:11:52.772 --> 01:11:54.230
So actually, it's
kind of-- what it

01:11:54.230 --> 01:11:59.300
tells you is that ellipsoids
are universal approximators

01:11:59.300 --> 01:12:02.525
of convex sets.

01:12:02.525 --> 01:12:04.150
Now, square root of
n and some practice

01:12:04.150 --> 01:12:07.400
might be way too much
for you to be happy with

01:12:07.400 --> 01:12:10.180
or something like that, but--

01:12:10.180 --> 01:12:12.960
actually, let me
do an example here.

01:12:12.960 --> 01:12:16.827
Let's just do one here.

01:12:16.827 --> 01:12:17.410
Here's my set.

01:12:17.410 --> 01:12:19.540
Ready?

01:12:19.540 --> 01:12:21.220
It's a triangle, like that.

01:12:24.760 --> 01:12:27.460
What's the Lowner-John
ellipsoid of that?

01:12:27.460 --> 01:12:30.310
It's a equilateral or
whatever they call it.

01:12:30.310 --> 01:12:32.440
What's the
Lowner-John ellipsoid?

01:12:32.440 --> 01:12:34.900
This is just a circle
that goes around it,

01:12:34.900 --> 01:12:36.387
so it looks like this.

01:12:36.387 --> 01:12:38.470
This is going to be terrible,
but OK, there it is.

01:12:40.990 --> 01:12:46.400
Shrink that by a factor of 2 in
your eye, and what do you get?

01:12:46.400 --> 01:12:48.205
You get this.

01:12:51.220 --> 01:12:54.340
And actually, this example
shows that that's tight,

01:12:54.340 --> 01:12:57.920
that inequality, and in
fact, it's always true.

01:12:57.920 --> 01:13:00.730
So you can even say something
very weird, like this.

01:13:00.730 --> 01:13:04.120
First you say, all convex
sets can be approximated

01:13:04.120 --> 01:13:06.390
by ellipsoids within a factor--

01:13:06.390 --> 01:13:07.510
you could say it's--

01:13:07.510 --> 01:13:09.130
I could say square
root n because I'd

01:13:09.130 --> 01:13:12.070
put it in the middle, and
then both expand and shrink.

01:13:12.070 --> 01:13:15.640
And you'd say, yeah, well,
what are the hardest sets

01:13:15.640 --> 01:13:17.790
to approximate by ellipsoids?

01:13:17.790 --> 01:13:19.900
Makes perfect sense, right?

01:13:19.900 --> 01:13:23.530
And the answer is,
a simplex in Rn.

01:13:23.530 --> 01:13:28.180
A simplex in Rn, if you want to
approximate it by ellipsoids,

01:13:28.180 --> 01:13:29.420
is going to take--

01:13:29.420 --> 01:13:31.720
it's going to take the
full square root n.

01:13:31.720 --> 01:13:33.730
Here would be my weird
square root n thing.

01:13:33.730 --> 01:13:35.630
It would be something
that looks like that.

01:13:35.630 --> 01:13:36.130
Whoops.

01:13:36.130 --> 01:13:37.390
OK, that came out very poorly.

01:13:37.390 --> 01:13:41.520
But anyway, that would be my--
this would be my square root n.

01:13:41.520 --> 01:13:46.170
And I would say, you see
that ball, that disk?

01:13:46.170 --> 01:13:50.310
If I increase it by
1.4, whatever, 1.42--

01:13:50.310 --> 01:13:54.490
there you go-- then
I cover my triangle.

01:13:54.490 --> 01:13:57.060
And if I shrink it, if
I multiply it by 0.7,

01:13:57.060 --> 01:13:58.500
then I'm inside.

01:13:58.500 --> 01:13:59.520
Everybody got that?

01:13:59.520 --> 01:14:02.970
And then-- so
everybody got all this?

01:14:02.970 --> 01:14:07.110
So this applies, actually,
weirdly, to norms.

01:14:07.110 --> 01:14:13.680
It says, take any norm
at all on Rn, any norm.

01:14:13.680 --> 01:14:16.530
It says that I can replace
it with a quadratic--

01:14:16.530 --> 01:14:18.720
I can approximate it
with a quadratic norm--

01:14:18.720 --> 01:14:20.250
those are symmetric--

01:14:20.250 --> 01:14:25.330
with error no more than
the fourth root of n.

01:14:25.330 --> 01:14:30.440
So now I'll tell you an
important thing about that.

01:14:30.440 --> 01:14:32.150
There's lots of norms.

01:14:32.150 --> 01:14:36.710
Here's a norm-- the
minimum fuel it takes you--

01:14:36.710 --> 01:14:41.570
the minimum fuel it takes
you to go from a state to 0--

01:14:41.570 --> 01:14:43.340
that's actually a norm.

01:14:43.340 --> 01:14:47.720
It's a crazy polyhedral norm
with lots of-- it's a crazy

01:14:47.720 --> 01:14:49.670
norm, but it is a norm.

01:14:49.670 --> 01:14:52.550
But that might be one
we really care about.

01:14:52.550 --> 01:14:55.070
It says I can
approximate that pretty

01:14:55.070 --> 01:14:56.450
well with a quadratic norm.

01:14:56.450 --> 01:14:57.570
Why does that matter?

01:14:57.570 --> 01:15:00.440
Because, in fact, the control
that people actually use

01:15:00.440 --> 01:15:04.110
is all based on quadratic stuff,
and so this would give you

01:15:04.110 --> 01:15:07.890
a very good way to find those--

01:15:07.890 --> 01:15:10.200
to find the-- actually,
it would actually

01:15:10.200 --> 01:15:14.947
say kind of why things work
so well when people would say,

01:15:14.947 --> 01:15:16.530
how are you controlling
your airplane?

01:15:16.530 --> 01:15:19.270
And if they're honest, finally
they'll say something like,

01:15:19.270 --> 01:15:21.450
well, it's basically
using something

01:15:21.450 --> 01:15:23.898
like linear
quadratic-- basically,

01:15:23.898 --> 01:15:25.440
it's almost least
squares, basically.

01:15:25.440 --> 01:15:27.630
It's more complicated
than that but not too far.

01:15:27.630 --> 01:15:29.310
And you go, whoa.

01:15:29.310 --> 01:15:32.244
You go, that's great.

01:15:32.244 --> 01:15:36.180
Are your actual constraints
spherical or whatever?

01:15:36.180 --> 01:15:37.770
And they're like, no, no.

01:15:37.770 --> 01:15:40.290
No, no, they're not,
that kind of thing.

01:15:40.290 --> 01:15:43.530
But it works really well, and
this is actually, at least

01:15:43.530 --> 01:15:46.905
I like to think, part
of the explanation.

01:15:46.905 --> 01:15:48.480
Does all this make sense?

01:15:48.480 --> 01:15:52.370
It's kind of weird and
interesting and cool, I think.

01:15:52.370 --> 01:15:54.020
OK.

01:15:54.020 --> 01:15:56.210
Next topic-- it's
something we've actually

01:15:56.210 --> 01:15:57.740
seen a couple of times before.

01:15:57.740 --> 01:16:00.530
It's so-called centering.

01:16:00.530 --> 01:16:03.440
So actually, very early in the
course, we encountered this.

01:16:03.440 --> 01:16:06.440
It was one of our first
examples of a linear program.

01:16:06.440 --> 01:16:09.440
It was like, here's a polyhedron
given by inequalities,

01:16:09.440 --> 01:16:15.110
and I said, find the largest
ball, Euclidean ball,

01:16:15.110 --> 01:16:16.290
that fits inside it.

01:16:16.290 --> 01:16:17.120
Everybody got that?

01:16:17.120 --> 01:16:19.340
That's called the
Chebyshev center.

01:16:19.340 --> 01:16:21.440
It's an LP, and I
said at the time

01:16:21.440 --> 01:16:25.820
it's weird because if I say
things like Euclidean ball

01:16:25.820 --> 01:16:29.990
or draw a circle, the linear
programming part of your brain

01:16:29.990 --> 01:16:33.703
should not be lighting up
because linear programming

01:16:33.703 --> 01:16:35.870
part of your brain should
light up when I say things

01:16:35.870 --> 01:16:39.500
like polyhedron, inequalities.

01:16:39.500 --> 01:16:42.900
Visually, you should be seeing
flat things with corners right.

01:16:42.900 --> 01:16:47.420
You should see a
modern fighter jet

01:16:47.420 --> 01:16:49.010
made with, like,
flat parts and stuff

01:16:49.010 --> 01:16:50.665
like that for various reasons.

01:16:50.665 --> 01:16:52.040
That's what you
should be seeing.

01:16:52.040 --> 01:16:55.280
You should not be
seeing beautiful curves.

01:16:55.280 --> 01:16:57.230
Anyway, that's what
you should be seeing.

01:16:57.230 --> 01:16:59.060
And so that was
weird, but that's it.

01:16:59.060 --> 01:17:00.950
OK, so that's the
Chebyshev center,

01:17:00.950 --> 01:17:02.510
but we have another
one immediately.

01:17:02.510 --> 01:17:03.468
Oh, we saw another one.

01:17:03.468 --> 01:17:07.430
We saw the yield center
or probability center.

01:17:07.430 --> 01:17:12.410
So that would be, take
a point, and then you're

01:17:12.410 --> 01:17:13.320
shifting the mean.

01:17:13.320 --> 01:17:16.770
Now put a 0 mean distribution
around it and with a log

01:17:16.770 --> 01:17:20.400
concave density, and
then maximize the-- pick

01:17:20.400 --> 01:17:22.740
the center that maximizes
the probability.

01:17:22.740 --> 01:17:23.580
That's another one.

01:17:23.580 --> 01:17:25.413
I think people call
it-- in different fields

01:17:25.413 --> 01:17:26.663
they call it different things.

01:17:26.663 --> 01:17:27.870
It's the yield center or--

01:17:27.870 --> 01:17:28.230
I don't know.

01:17:28.230 --> 01:17:29.647
There's all sorts
of names for it.

01:17:29.647 --> 01:17:32.370
Everybody-- or
probabilistic center.

01:17:32.370 --> 01:17:34.770
But here's another one, the
center for the maximum volume

01:17:34.770 --> 01:17:42.540
ellipsoid, and that's actually
a very good one for reasons

01:17:42.540 --> 01:17:44.325
I'll get to but not today.

01:17:46.970 --> 01:17:49.190
So this is centering.

01:17:49.190 --> 01:17:52.820
It turns out, of course--
look, centering-- let's talk

01:17:52.820 --> 01:17:54.660
about centering in
R. It's going to be

01:17:54.660 --> 01:17:56.480
a very short conversation.

01:17:56.480 --> 01:17:58.910
So here's a convex set in
R. It's called an interval.

01:17:58.910 --> 01:18:00.650
What's the center
of an interval?

01:18:00.650 --> 01:18:01.520
A bounded interval.

01:18:01.520 --> 01:18:04.873
What's the center
of the interval?

01:18:04.873 --> 01:18:05.540
It's the center.

01:18:05.540 --> 01:18:06.540
It's the middle of it.

01:18:06.540 --> 01:18:06.770
Come on.

01:18:06.770 --> 01:18:07.560
That's what it is.

01:18:07.560 --> 01:18:08.560
And there's no other--

01:18:08.560 --> 01:18:09.560
there's no other reason.

01:18:09.560 --> 01:18:11.560
You say, no, I think it
should be the one that's

01:18:11.560 --> 01:18:14.180
one third of the way towards
the left, and you're like,

01:18:14.180 --> 01:18:16.460
what does that mean?

01:18:16.460 --> 01:18:20.395
But the minute you get to R2,
it's actually interesting.

01:18:20.395 --> 01:18:22.520
It turns out there's multiple
different definitions

01:18:22.520 --> 01:18:26.480
of a center, and we're going
to look at some of them,

01:18:26.480 --> 01:18:28.490
so just to point this out.

01:18:28.490 --> 01:18:31.070
OK.

01:18:31.070 --> 01:18:34.970
One thing we-- one thing
that's going to come up later

01:18:34.970 --> 01:18:36.560
is the so-called
analytic center,

01:18:36.560 --> 01:18:38.630
and it's actually a weird thing.

01:18:38.630 --> 01:18:40.700
It's a bit--

01:18:40.700 --> 01:18:44.480
I'll say why it's weird in a
minute, but it's actually not--

01:18:44.480 --> 01:18:47.510
it's not a center of
the geometrical object.

01:18:47.510 --> 01:18:49.903
It's actually of a
set of inequalities.

01:18:49.903 --> 01:18:52.070
So if I have a set of
inequalities and some equality

01:18:52.070 --> 01:18:55.730
constraint, if I minimize the--

01:18:55.730 --> 01:18:59.120
if I maximize the sum of the
logs-- these are the margins--

01:18:59.120 --> 01:19:01.100
fi should be less
than 0, so minus fi

01:19:01.100 --> 01:19:04.140
should be positive
or non-negative.

01:19:04.140 --> 01:19:07.170
But here this says,
basically, maximize

01:19:07.170 --> 01:19:09.990
the product of the margins.

01:19:09.990 --> 01:19:14.418
That's called the analytic
center of these inequalities,

01:19:14.418 --> 01:19:16.710
and it's going to turn out
that's very easily computed.

01:19:16.710 --> 01:19:18.600
As a matter of fact,
you will be computing it

01:19:18.600 --> 01:19:23.880
in about two weeks, so you'll
be writing code to do that.

01:19:23.880 --> 01:19:27.420
Anyway, I think what we'll do
is we'll quit here for today,

01:19:27.420 --> 01:19:30.740
and we'll continue next time.

